(window.TPR_CH = window.TPR_CH || []).push(
{"id":"c-s-01","n":1201,"prog":"Adult Psychiatry","icon":"🧩","title":"Fourth overdose this year: is this emotionally unstable personality disorder?","src":"","moment":{"when":"Saturday 11:40","where":"Emergency department, liaison psychiatry","text":"A 22-year-old woman took 16 paracetamol tablets with wine after a row with her boyfriend this morning, then rang 999 herself. Her 4-hour level was below the treatment line and the medical team have cleared her. It is her fourth overdose this year. She says, 'I've told everyone already. I just want to go home.'"},"task":"Take a diagnostic history covering emotional instability, self-image, relationships, impulsivity and self-harm; exclude depressive, bipolar and psychotic disorders; assess risk briefly; and explain the working diagnosis in plain words.","first60":["Read the ED notes and previous liaison letters: dates, amounts and triggers of past overdoses, and any previous diagnosis.","Check the medical clearance: paracetamol level, timing, LFTs and INR, and that no treatment is still running.","Ask the nurse how she has been on the department: distressed, angry, settled, any attempt to leave.","Find a quiet room, sit at her level, and plan to explain why this conversation matters before asking anything.","Keep the structure in your head: today, emotions, self-image, relationships, impulses and self-harm, then what it is not."],"ask":["'Can you take me through what happened this morning, from the start?'","'When your mood changes, how fast does it happen, and how long does it last?'","'How would you describe yourself as a person? Does that change a lot?'","'What are your relationships usually like? How do you feel if someone might leave?'","'Do you act on impulse in ways you later regret?'","'Apart from overdoses, do you hurt yourself in any other way?'","'Have you had two weeks or more of feeling low every day, or days of feeling high with little need for sleep?'","'Have you heard voices from outside your head, or felt people were against you?'"],"recognise":["Mood that flips within minutes to hours, set off by rejection or conflict, then settles: this is affective instability, not a mood episode.","Idealise then devalue, frantic efforts to avoid being left, and a self-image that shifts with whoever she is with.","Impulsivity in at least two areas (spending, alcohol, drugs, damage) plus repeated self-harm used to release tension.","Stress-linked paranoia or numb, unreal feelings that pass quickly are part of the pattern; voices from outside and fixed false beliefs are not.","The pattern started in her teens and shows up across work, study and relationships: that is what makes it a personality pattern.","Childhood loss or care history is common; note it, do not force it today."],"decide":[{"c":"Complete the history and brief risk assessment, explain a working diagnosis of EUPD kindly, agree a written crisis plan, and refer to the community team for assessment for structured therapy, with a GP letter.","r":"Right: it answers her question honestly, keeps her safe without needless admission, and opens the door to the treatment that works.","best":true},{"c":"Admit her to the ward overnight because this is her fourth overdose.","r":"Usually unhelpful: she is no longer suicidal, has a protective plan and a friend collecting her. Unplanned admission can increase distress and dependence; brief admission is for crisis only.","best":false},{"c":"Start an antidepressant and a low-dose antipsychotic for her mood swings and paranoia.","r":"Wrong: she has no depressive episode or psychosis, and NICE advises against drug treatment for the personality disorder itself. Paracetamol access also matters more than a new prescription.","best":false}],"law":["She has capacity and is informal: she may leave. If she tries to go before the assessment ends, assess capacity for that decision and ask for senior help; do not hold her without a legal basis.","NICE NG225 (self-harm, 2022): offer a full psychosocial assessment after every episode; risk tools and scales should not be used to predict suicide or to decide discharge.","Confidentiality: share the plan with the GP; ask before involving her friend or boyfriend.","Safeguarding: ask about violence in relationships and whether any children or adults at risk live with her."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I know you've been asked a lot today. I'm asking because this is the fourth time this year, and I want the help you get to actually fit what's going on."},{"to":"Patient","s":"What you've described has a name. It doesn't mean you're bad or mad. It means your feelings fire up fast and strong and take a while to settle, and there's therapy that really helps."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Working diagnosis: emotionally unstable personality disorder, borderline type. No depressive episode or psychosis. Referred to CMHT for assessment for DBT. Please avoid large prescriptions of paracetamol or other medicines that are dangerous in overdose."}],"write":"Liaison psychiatry. 22F. 4th overdose in 12 months. Medically cleared (4-h paracetamol level below line).\nToday: row with boyfriend by phone, ~half bottle of wine, 16 x 500 mg paracetamol, wanted to die at the time, rang 999 after ~40 min (worried about cats). Now regrets, no current intent.\nPattern since age ~14: mood switches within minutes-hours, triggered by rejection; frequent anger; chronic emptiness; unstable self-image; intense idealise-devalue relationships; fear of abandonment; impulsive spending, caution for criminal damage; alcohol ~30-40 units/wk (binges); cannabis weekends, occasional MDMA; cuts forearms weekly to relieve tension; brief stress-linked paranoid ideas and depersonalisation.\nNo sustained depressive episode; no hypomania (highs last <1 h); no hallucinations or delusions.\nBackground: foster care from 11 (did not wish to discuss). Mutual physical violence with ex-partner; current partner shouts, no violence. No children.\nImpression: EUPD, borderline type (ICD-11 PD with borderline pattern). Risk of repeat self-harm high, especially after relationship conflict and alcohol.\nPlan: diagnosis explained, teach-back good. Crisis plan agreed; friend removing paracetamol; alcohol advice. Discharge with friend. CMHT referral for structured psychological therapy (DBT). GP letter. Crisis numbers given.\nDr A. Iqbal CT2","make":["Earn the conversation first: name her frustration and give a reason for talking before asking a single question.","Pin down duration: mood that shifts in minutes to hours is a different thing from a low or high that lasts days or weeks.","Ask for real examples: 'What did you spend?', 'What happened next?' Examples turn vague answers into evidence.","Avoid jargon: say 'feeling empty inside' and 'scared people will leave', not 'chronic emptiness' or 'abandonment fears'.","Keep risk brief but complete: now, the future, and violence in relationships.","Use the diagnosis to give hope and direction, never as a label that closes the door."],"next":"She leaves with her friend, who takes the remaining paracetamol home. The CMHT assesses her three weeks later and offers a place in a DBT programme. Two months on she has not taken another overdose and has started using a skills app when rows with her boyfriend flare.","marks":["Explained why the conversation mattered and gained her agreement.","Clarified the overdose: trigger, amount, intent and rescue.","Covered emotions, anger, emptiness, self-image, relationships, impulsivity and self-harm, with examples.","Distinguished brief mood shifts from depressive and hypomanic episodes.","Separated stress-linked paranoia from psychosis.","Explained the working diagnosis without jargon, with hope, and agreed a crisis plan."],"pitfalls":["Calling her 'attention-seeking' or 'a frequent flyer', in words or tone.","Diagnosing bipolar disorder because she says her mood goes up and down.","Reading a list of criteria at her instead of asking for examples.","Leaving out violence in relationships and substance use.","Discharging without removing access to the remaining tablets."],"refs":["1. NICE CG78 Borderline personality disorder: recognition and management (2009; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG225 Self-harm: assessment, management and preventing recurrence (2022; checked Nov 2026)","3. WHO ICD-11: personality disorder and borderline pattern specifier (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: Borderline personality disorder (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK services avoid labels like 'manipulative' or 'PD patient'. Many UK records still use EUPD, while ICD-11 uses 'personality disorder with borderline pattern'. People with this diagnosis often say they were dismissed before; respect is itself part of the treatment.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-11","n":1211,"prog":"Core: first weeks and on call","icon":"🩸","title":"Annual bloods on clozapine: explain metabolic syndrome, keep the clozapine, treat the body","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 10:30","where":"Rehabilitation physical health clinic, community rehabilitation team","text":"Mr Martin Hale, a 47-year-old man with schizophrenia, has been well on clozapine 400 mg a day for 15 years and lives in supported accommodation. His annual results are back: waist 108 cm, blood pressure 146/92, fasting glucose 6.4 mmol/L, HbA1c 45 mmol/mol, HDL 0.8 mmol/L, triglycerides 2.7 mmol/L. Last week he read that people with schizophrenia die 20 years early. He says, 'Please don't take my clozapine away.'"},"task":"Explain his results as metabolic syndrome, check his cardiovascular risk factors, and agree lifestyle support, metformin, a statin and GP actions while keeping clozapine.","first60":["Line up this year's results against last year's: weight, waist, BP, fasting glucose, HbA1c, lipids, clozapine level.","Check the GP record for a QRISK3 score, a hypertension diagnosis and any medicines he has turned down.","Check the smoking history: clozapine levels depend on it, so any quit plan changes the dose.","Check renal function and LFTs are on file before suggesting metformin or a statin.","Have the clinic tape measure, the NHS Healthier You leaflet and a blank written plan ready."],"ask":["'What did the article say, and what worries you most?'","'How many cigarettes a day? Are you thinking of stopping, and how?'","'Has anyone in your family had a heart attack, stroke or diabetes, and at what age?'","'The GP mentioned your blood pressure. What happened about that?'","'Are you very thirsty, passing more urine, or getting up at night to pass water?'","'Talk me through what you ate yesterday. Who does the cooking?'","'How much do you move in a normal day? What gets in the way?'","'Any alcohol or other drugs?'"],"recognise":["Waist over 94 cm plus at least two of: high triglycerides, low HDL, BP 130/85 or more, fasting glucose 5.6 or more. He has all four: metabolic syndrome.","Fasting glucose 6.4 and HbA1c 45 mean non-diabetic hyperglycaemia (prediabetes), not diabetes yet.","Clozapine carries the highest metabolic risk of all antipsychotics, through hunger, weight gain and insulin resistance.","Smoking speeds clozapine breakdown. Stopping suddenly can push levels up within days: drowsiness, drooling, jerks, even fits.","He has treatment-resistant schizophrenia and has been well for 15 years: switching carries a risk of relapse and is a last resort.","Clozapine can rarely cause sudden severe hyperglycaemia or diabetic ketoacidosis, so he needs warning signs."],"decide":[{"c":"Keep clozapine 400 mg. Explain metabolic syndrome, refer to Healthier You and the dietitian, offer metformin and atorvastatin 20 mg, ask the GP to confirm BP and treat it, and plan clozapine levels before he stops smoking.","r":"Right: every abnormal result has its own action, the drug that keeps him well stays, and the smoking interaction is made safe.","best":true},{"c":"Switch clozapine to aripiprazole to improve his metabolic profile.","r":"Wrong first move: two earlier antipsychotics failed and he is well. Switching risks relapse; it is a last resort only if all else fails.","best":false},{"c":"Back his plan to stop smoking cold turkey on Monday, and leave everything else to the GP.","r":"Unsafe: his clozapine level could rise sharply within a week, and leaving it all to the GP drops the psychiatrist's own responsibility.","best":false}],"law":["NICE CG178: people on antipsychotics need an annual physical health check (weight, waist, BP, glucose or HbA1c, lipids, smoking), and secondary care keeps responsibility until handover is agreed.","NICE NG238: offer atorvastatin 20 mg for primary prevention when QRISK3 is 10% or more after discussing lifestyle.","NICE NG136: a clinic BP of 140/90 or above needs confirming with ambulatory or home monitoring before diagnosis.","GMC Decision making and consent: give the risks of each option, including doing nothing, and respect his choice."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Let me say this first: I've no plans to stop your clozapine. It's kept you well for 15 years. Today is about looking after your heart and your sugar alongside it."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Stopping smoking is one of the best things you could do. But please don't stop on Monday without us. When you stop, your clozapine level can climb fast, so we'll check it and adjust the dose with you."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Metabolic syndrome on clozapine. Please confirm BP with home monitoring and treat if confirmed, refer to the NHS Diabetes Prevention Programme, and continue metformin and atorvastatin, which we have started with his agreement."}],"write":"Rehab physical health clinic. 47M, treatment-resistant schizophrenia, well on clozapine 400 mg/day (150 mg mane, 250 mg nocte) for 15 yrs. Level 0.48 mg/L.\nResults: wt 104 kg (+6 kg/2 yrs), BMI 33, waist 108 cm, BP 146/92, FPG 6.4, HbA1c 45, TC 6.1, HDL 0.8, TG 2.7, eGFR >90, LFTs normal. QRISK3 21% (GP).\nRisk factors: smokes 30/day, planned abrupt stop Monday. Father stroke age 70. Declined antihypertensive in past. No alcohol or drugs. Inactive, constant hunger. No polyuria or polydipsia.\nImpression: metabolic syndrome, non-diabetic hyperglycaemia, likely clozapine-related plus smoking and inactivity.\nDiscussed: keep clozapine; switching last resort. Teach-back correct.\nPlan: quit date moved to 2/52 with Stop Smoking Service; clozapine level pre-quit and weekly x4 after; dose review by team. Metformin 500 mg OD with breakfast, titrate. Atorvastatin 20 mg ON. Dietitian, Healthier You, walking group. GP: confirm BP (HBPM). DKA warning signs given. Written plan given. Review 4/52.\nDr K. Mensah CT2","make":["Lead with the reassurance he needs: clozapine stays. Then explain each result in one plain sentence.","Join the results into one picture (metabolic syndrome) before planning, then give one action per result.","Never let a clozapine patient stop or restart smoking without a level and a dose plan.","Give numbers that mean something to him: waist under 94 cm, BP under 140/90, HbA1c under 42.","Use 'we' and polite suggestion; ask what he can realistically change with little money and no kitchen skills.","Write the plan down for him and for his support workers, and copy the GP."],"next":"He moves his quit date back two weeks and starts NRT patches with the stop-smoking adviser. His clozapine level rises from 0.48 to 0.71 mg/L in the first week; the team trims the dose and it settles. Three months on he has lost 4 kg and his HbA1c is 43.","marks":["Reassured him early that clozapine would continue.","Took a full cardiovascular risk history, including family history and smoking.","Explained the results as metabolic syndrome in plain words.","Spotted the smoking and clozapine interaction and planned levels.","Offered metformin and a statin with the checks each needs.","Gave high blood sugar warning signs and wrote to the GP."],"pitfalls":["Opening with 'we may need to change your clozapine'.","Reading out numbers without saying what they mean.","Missing the smoking interaction, or praising a cold-turkey quit.","Handing everything to the GP with no plan from the mental health team.","Lecturing instead of asking what he can manage."],"refs":["1. NICE CG178 Psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (2014; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG238 Cardiovascular disease: risk assessment and reduction, including lipid modification (2023; checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE NG136 Hypertension in adults: diagnosis and management (checked Nov 2026)","4. Cooper SJ et al. BAP guidelines on weight gain, metabolic disturbance and cardiovascular risk with psychosis and antipsychotics. J Psychopharmacol 2016","5. BNF: clozapine, metformin, atorvastatin (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK the mental health team often holds the antipsychotic prescription while the GP runs long-term physical care, so a clear letter saying who does what is part of the job. The Lester tool is the usual guide to physical health checks on antipsychotics.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-12","n":1212,"prog":"Core: first weeks and on call","icon":"⚖️","title":"Well on olanzapine but 12 kg heavier: agree a weight plan without losing the drug that works","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 14:45","where":"Outpatient clinic, community mental health team","text":"Mr Adam Clarke, a 35-year-old man with paranoid schizophrenia, restarted olanzapine 10 mg at night six months ago after a relapse. He has been free of symptoms for three months, but has put on 12 kg. Two years ago he stopped olanzapine because of weight gain and relapsed. He says, 'I'll keep taking it, but I can't keep getting bigger.'"},"task":"Discuss his weight gain on olanzapine, check his metabolic monitoring, and agree diet, activity, a formal programme and the place of metformin, aripiprazole or a switch, with follow-up.","first60":["Plot his weights since olanzapine started; note the 12-week bloods (HbA1c, lipids) and BP.","List every antipsychotic tried, for how long, at what dose, and why it stopped.","Note the last relapse: what happened after he stopped olanzapine before.","Have a tape measure and the BP machine ready; you need waist and BP today.","Know the add-on options and their typical effect: aripiprazole about 2 kg, metformin about 3 kg."],"ask":["'How have you been in yourself lately? Any voices or worries about people?'","'When did the weight start going on, and how much?'","'Which other medicines have you tried, and how did you get on with them?'","'How is your appetite? What does a normal day's eating look like?'","'How many hours do you sleep? How active are you?'","'What have you tried to lose weight? What got in the way?'","'Any breathlessness, chest pain, snoring or joint pain?'","'Do you smoke or vape? How much do you drink? Any heart problems or diabetes in the family?'"],"recognise":["Olanzapine is one of the highest-risk antipsychotics for weight gain, mainly through hunger and more eating; most is gained in the first months.","He has failed five other antipsychotics and relapsed after stopping olanzapine: switching is not the right first answer.","Unhappiness with weight is a common reason people stop antipsychotics; tackling it protects against relapse.","Breathlessness, ten hours' sleep and weight gain: ask about snoring and daytime sleepiness for sleep apnoea.","A father with a heart attack at 65 and past smoking add to his cardiovascular risk.","Group or structured programmes do better than going it alone; each add-on medicine gives modest loss."],"decide":[{"c":"Keep olanzapine 10 mg. Agree specific food and activity changes, refer to a structured weight programme, step up monitoring, and offer metformin (or aripiprazole 5 mg add-on) if weight is still rising at 3 months.","r":"Right: the drug that works stays, lifestyle comes first with proper support, and add-on options are explained with honest, modest numbers.","best":true},{"c":"Switch to aripiprazole monotherapy because it is weight-neutral.","r":"Wrong for him: aripiprazole alone did not control his illness before. A switch risks a third relapse.","best":false},{"c":"Reduce olanzapine to 5 mg to slow the weight gain.","r":"Risky: 10 mg is his effective dose after a recent relapse. Any reduction needs a separate, cautious discussion, not a weight fix.","best":false}],"law":["NICE CG178: monitor weight weekly for 6 weeks, then at 12 weeks, 1 year and yearly; HbA1c or fasting glucose, lipids, BP and pulse at 12 weeks, 1 year and yearly. Offer a healthy eating and activity programme.","BAP 2016 guidance: lifestyle first; consider metformin or aripiprazole add-on when weight gain persists and a switch is not suitable.","GMC Decision making and consent: discuss each option, including doing nothing, with its benefits and risks.","Metformin for antipsychotic weight gain without diabetes is off-label: record the discussion."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"You've worked hard to get well, and it's completely fair to want your body back too. I'm not going to suggest a switch: the olanzapine works for you. Let's tackle the weight around it."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Group programmes beat going it alone. People lose more weight with that structure and support, and it's not about being lectured."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Olanzapine 10 mg continues; 12 kg gain over 6 months. Please refer to the local weight management service. If weight is still rising at 3 months, we recommend metformin, starting 500 mg daily with food; please check eGFR first."}],"write":"CMHT clinic. 35M, paranoid schizophrenia. Olanzapine 10 mg nocte restarted 6/12 ago after relapse; symptom free 3/12. No psychotic features today; insight good.\nMeds tried: risperidone, quetiapine (ineffective), aripiprazole, amisulpride, haloperidol (less effective). Stopped olanzapine 2 yrs ago due to weight: relapsed.\nWeight: 70 kg before first olanzapine; now 88 kg (+12 kg in 6/12). BMI 27.8. Waist 101 cm. BP 132/84, P 78. 12-week HbA1c 40, non-HDL 4.1.\nAppetite up, snacks crisps/chocolate, larger portions. Sleeps 10 h. Walks 3 km 3x/week. Breathless on stairs; no chest pain; partner reports snoring. Vapes (ex-smoker 10 yrs). Alcohol 6 units/week. Father MI at 65.\nPlan: continue olanzapine 10 mg. Diet and activity changes agreed. Referred to weight programme. Weight monthly, HbA1c and lipids now and at 3/12. STOP-BANG done: GP to consider sleep study. Metformin or aripiprazole add-on if still gaining at 3/12. Written plan given. Review 3/12.\nDr R. Patel CT3","make":["Establish the timeline: weight before, during and off olanzapine, and what each other drug did.","Name it plainly: a common side effect, driven by appetite, not his fault.","Give concrete food advice: set meals, no grazing, protein and high-fibre foods, smaller plates, junk out of the house.","Say why a group programme works better, and offer a private or online option if he feels embarrassed.","Give add-on numbers honestly: metformin about 3 kg, aripiprazole about 2 kg on average.","Book the monitoring: weight monthly for now, HbA1c and lipids today and at 3 months."],"next":"He joins the trust's healthy-living group and uses the NHS Weight Loss Plan app. At three months he has lost 2 kg, but weight is creeping back. The GP starts metformin; at six months he is 5 kg down and still well on olanzapine.","marks":["Took a brief history of the illness and the medicines tried.","Linked the weight gain in time to olanzapine.","Screened for health effects and checked monitoring.","Gave specific diet and activity advice.","Recommended a structured programme and explained why.","Explained why a switch is not advised; discussed metformin and aripiprazole honestly."],"pitfalls":["Suggesting a switch for a man who has failed five other antipsychotics.","Generic 'eat less, move more' with no specifics.","Skipping the physical health screen or the monitoring.","Overselling add-on medicines.","Missing a snoring and sleepiness question when weight and sleep are up."],"refs":["1. NICE CG178 Psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (checked Nov 2026)","2. Cooper SJ et al. BAP guidelines on weight gain, metabolic disturbance and cardiovascular risk with psychosis and antipsychotics. J Psychopharmacol 2016","3. NICE PH38 on preventing type 2 diabetes in high-risk adults (checked Nov 2026)","4. BNF: olanzapine, metformin, aripiprazole (checked Nov 2026)","5. NHS website: NHS Weight Loss Plan (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in UK clinics the psychiatrist often recommends metformin and the GP prescribes it, so the letter must say exactly what you advise and why. Patients usually get a copy.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-14","n":1214,"prog":"Core: first weeks and on call","icon":"🌡️","title":"Her son was moved to the medical ward overnight: explain NMS to his angry mother","src":"","moment":{"when":"Saturday 10:15","where":"Relatives' room, acute psychiatric ward","text":"Ryan Daley, a 24-year-old man with schizoaffective disorder detained under Section 2, was restarted on haloperidol and increased to 6 mg a day over four days. Last night he became stiff, confused and feverish (38.6°C, pulse 128) with a CK of 6,200. He was moved to the acute medical unit with neuroleptic malignant syndrome. His mother, Mrs Carol Daley, 51, was phoned at 3 am and told only 'a side effect'. She has arrived on the ward, furious. Ryan has agreed that everything can be shared with her."},"task":"Explain what neuroleptic malignant syndrome is, why it happened, the treatment, the outlook and how antipsychotics will be restarted safely, and agree how she will be kept updated.","first60":["Ring the medical unit for an update today: temperature, pulse, CK trend, kidney function, how alert he is.","Confirm Ryan's consent to share in the notes, and check his Section 17 leave and escort arrangements.","Read the drug chart: haloperidol doses and timing, any IM medicines, fluid intake before he became unwell.","Find a private room and ask the nurse who knows Ryan to join you.","Have the ward phone number, the consultant's name and the PALS leaflet to hand."],"ask":["'What have you been told so far?'","'What's worrying you most right now?'","'What questions would you like answered before you leave?'","'How would you like to be kept updated, and how often?'"],"recognise":["NMS: a rare, unpredictable, potentially life-threatening reaction to antipsychotics: rigidity, fever, autonomic instability (fast pulse, swinging BP) and confusion, with a raised CK.","Risk factors include a rapid dose increase, high-potency drugs such as haloperidol, IM medicines, dehydration, agitation and previous NMS.","Treatment: stop the antipsychotic, IV fluids, cooling, benzodiazepines, close monitoring of kidneys and CK; dantrolene or bromocriptine in severe cases, under the medical team.","Complications include kidney injury from muscle breakdown, blood clots, chest infection and heart rhythm problems.","After oral antipsychotics most people recover within one to two weeks; recovery after a depot takes longer.","An angry relative given no information needs an apology for that and openness, not defensiveness."],"decide":[{"c":"Apologise for the poor communication, explain NMS plainly with an honest outlook, describe how antipsychotics will be restarted later, and agree a named contact and daily updates.","r":"Right: honest, open, plain explanation that answers her four worries and rebuilds trust.","best":true},{"c":"Say you can't discuss it until the consultant is back on Monday.","r":"Wrong: Ryan has consented, she is frightened, and delay fuels anger. Explain what you know now and arrange the consultant meeting too.","best":false},{"c":"Tell her antipsychotics will never be used again.","r":"Inaccurate: most people can take an antipsychotic again later with a careful plan. Promising otherwise sets up the next relapse.","best":false}],"law":["Ryan has capacity and has consented to full sharing: GMC Confidentiality (2017) allows open discussion with his mother.","Professional duty of candour (GMC and NMC): be open and honest when care causes harm, and apologise. The trust decides if the statutory duty also applies.","Section 17 leave: a detained patient moved to a general hospital needs leave authorised by his responsible clinician; Section 2 continues.","Report serious suspected adverse drug reactions to the MHRA through the Yellow Card scheme."],"say":[{"to":"Mother","s":"I'm really sorry you were phoned at 3 am and given so little. You should have been told more, and I'll put that right now."},{"to":"Mother","s":"He's had a rare but serious reaction to his medicine. It's being treated in the right place, the medical team says he is responding well, and most people recover fully."},{"to":"Medical team","s":"Ryan's mother is fully informed with his consent. Could the medical registrar call her after the ward round today? Our liaison team will review his mental state daily."}],"write":"Meeting with Mrs C. Daley (mother, nearest relative) in relatives' room with staff nurse J. Price. Ryan's consent to full sharing documented (capacity present for this decision, 07:30 today).\nUpdate from AMU 09:45: T 37.6, P 104, BP stable, CK 6,200 to 4,100, creatinine normal, alert. IV fluids, cooling, lorazepam. Septic screen negative so far.\nExplained: NMS, features, likely contributors (rapid haloperidol titration, IM lorazepam use, poor fluid intake), treatment, complications, outlook, future antipsychotic plan. Apologised for poor overnight communication.\nHer concerns: what happened; recovery; future medication; updates. All addressed; teach-back correct.\nPlan: daily 16:00 call from ward (named nurse J. Price); consultant meeting Monday 14:00; liaison psychiatry daily review on AMU; haloperidol stopped and recorded as an adverse reaction; Yellow Card and incident report done. PALS details given.\nDr F. Osei CT1","make":["Start by letting her say what she has been told and what she fears; then apologise for the gap.","Use plain words: 'a rare reaction where the muscles become stiff, the temperature rises and the body's automatic controls go haywire'.","Be honest about why: a quick dose rise is a known risk factor, and it will be reviewed.","Answer her four questions explicitly: what happened, will he recover, can he take medicine again, who will update her.","Give the future plan: wait until fully recovered, a different medicine, low dose, slow increase, close checks.","Leave her with a name, a time and a number."],"next":"Ryan's CK is normal by day six and he returns to the psychiatric ward on day nine. Two weeks later, aripiprazole is started at a low dose with daily temperature, pulse and BP checks and CK twice weekly. His mother joins the review and gets a daily call during the first week.","marks":["Let her speak first and apologised for poor communication.","Explained NMS in plain words, including its seriousness.","Was honest about likely contributing factors.","Explained the treatment and an honest outlook.","Described how antipsychotics will be restarted safely.","Agreed a named contact and update times."],"pitfalls":["Becoming defensive or blaming the medical team.","Hiding behind confidentiality when consent is documented.","Using jargon: 'autonomic instability', 'rhabdomyolysis', 'CK'.","Promising he will never need antipsychotics again, or that nothing went wrong.","Ending without saying who will call her and when."],"refs":["1. BNF: Antipsychotic drugs, neuroleptic malignant syndrome (checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG178 Psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (checked Nov 2026)","3. GMC and NMC: Openness and honesty when things go wrong: the professional duty of candour (2015, updated 2022)","4. GMC Confidentiality: good practice in handling patient information (2017)","5. MHRA Yellow Card scheme (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, apologising is not an admission of liability, and families expect honesty when treatment causes harm. PALS (Patient Advice and Liaison Service) is the usual first stop if a family wants to raise concerns.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-22","n":1222,"prog":"Core: first weeks and on call","icon":"📈","title":"Her ECG shows QTc 520 ms after a quetiapine increase","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 11:40","where":"Community mental health team outpatient clinic","text":"Irene Walsh, 74, has had schizophrenia since her thirties. Five weeks ago her consultant increased quetiapine MR from 400 mg to 600 mg at night after her voices returned. She also takes indapamide 2.5 mg each morning and metformin 500 mg twice daily. Today's routine ECG, done by the healthcare assistant, reports QTc 520 ms; eight months ago it was 446 ms. Bloods taken last week: potassium 3.2 mmol/L, corrected calcium 2.04 mmol/L, magnesium not done. She feels well. Her daughter Karen is in the waiting room."},"task":"Read the ECG, calculate and explain the QTc, check her symptoms and risk factors (electrolytes, drugs, cardiac and family history) and agree a safe dose and monitoring plan.","first60":["Check the ECG yourself before you see her: is the machine's QT right, and is the rate right?","Recalculate the QTc: QT divided by the square root of the RR interval in seconds.","Above 500 ms is the action line: same-day medical advice and stop the likely cause.","List every QT-prolonging factor: drugs, low potassium, calcium or magnesium, age, sex, heart disease.","Tell her clearly that the tracing is abnormal, then explain in plain words."],"ask":["'Any fluttering, racing heart, dizzy spells, blackouts, chest pain or breathlessness?'","'Have you ever had a heart problem? Has anyone in your family died suddenly when young?'","'What tablets are you on, including anything from the chemist, a shop, or the GP recently?'","'Could you have taken an extra dose by mistake?'","'Any vomiting or diarrhoea lately?' (both lower potassium and magnesium)","'How have the voices been since the increase?'"],"recognise":["QTc above 500 ms carries a clearly raised risk of torsades de pointes, which can cause fainting and sudden death.","Bazett's formula overcorrects at fast heart rates; Fridericia's (cube root) is more accurate above about 90 bpm. At 75 bpm, both give a value at or above 500 here.","Quetiapine has a moderate, dose-related effect on QTc; aripiprazole has very little.","Macrolide antibiotics (clarithromycin, erythromycin), citalopram, methadone, ondansetron and many others add to the risk.","Low potassium, calcium and magnesium all lengthen the QT; thiazide-like diuretics such as indapamide lower potassium.","Older age, female sex, bradycardia and structural heart disease are fixed risk factors."],"decide":[{"c":"Hold quetiapine today, phone the medical registrar for same-day assessment and electrolyte replacement, then restart at the old dose only when QTc has settled.","r":"Removes the main cause, treats the reversible factors under monitoring and keeps her on an antipsychotic she trusts.","best":true},{"c":"Reduce quetiapine to 400 mg and repeat the ECG at her next routine visit.","r":"Too slow: at 520 ms with low potassium she needs the same-day medical review and monitoring.","best":false},{"c":"Stop quetiapine permanently and start haloperidol, which is effective in older adults.","r":"Haloperidol has a strong QTc effect; switching to it makes things worse.","best":false}],"law":["Consent: she has capacity and agrees to same-day assessment; explain risks and options so her decision is informed (GMC Decision making and consent, 2020).","Duty of care: if she refused, explain the risk, document her capacity and the advice given, and give a clear safety net; do not detain under the MHA for a physical problem.","Report suspected adverse drug reactions such as QT prolongation through the MHRA Yellow Card scheme."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"After each beat your heart has to recharge. Yours is taking longer than it should, and that can occasionally trigger a dangerous rhythm. Acting today makes that much less likely."},{"to":"Medical registrar","s":"74-year-old woman on quetiapine MR 600 mg, QTc 520 ms by Bazett, previously 446. Asymptomatic, sinus rhythm 75. Potassium 3.2, corrected calcium 2.04, recent clarithromycin. Quetiapine held. Please see in SDEC today for monitoring and electrolyte replacement."},{"to":"Patient (her fear)","s":"I'm not taking you off treatment for good. We're making it safe first, then restarting at a dose your heart was fine on."}],"write":"Outpatient review: ECG.\nECG (my reading): sinus rhythm 75 bpm, PR 160 ms, QRS 88 ms, normal axis, flat T waves with U waves V2–V3, no ST change. QT 465 ms, RR 0.80 s, QTc (Bazett) 520 ms, (Fridericia) 501 ms. Previous QTc 446 ms (8 months ago, quetiapine 400 mg).\nSymptoms: none (no palpitations, syncope, chest pain). No cardiac or family history of sudden death.\nRisk factors: quetiapine increased to 600 mg 5 weeks ago; clarithromycin finished 3 days ago; K 3.2, corrected Ca 2.04; indapamide; age 74, female.\nDiscussed with consultant Dr Rahman: quetiapine held from tonight.\nMedical registrar (on call) accepted to SDEC today; daughter driving her now.\nPlan: restart quetiapine MR 400 mg only when QTc < 470 ms and electrolytes normal; ECG 1 week after restart; aripiprazole if QTc stays long. GP letter: review indapamide, check magnesium and vitamin D. CPN weekly for relapse signs. Yellow Card submitted.\nSafety net: 999 for palpitations, faint or chest pain.","make":["Phone the on-call medical registrar and agree same-day assessment (SDEC or ED).","Phone the consultant and record the decision to hold quetiapine.","Write to the GP: indapamide review, magnesium, calcium and vitamin D check.","Ask the CPN for weekly visits for relapse signs while the dose changes.","Book a repeat ECG one week after any restart or switch, and submit a Yellow Card."],"next":"Three weeks later her QTc is 452 ms on quetiapine MR 400 mg with normal potassium, but Sue reports Irene is hearing whispering again at night. Review her mental state, discuss the options (aripiprazole added or switched, or psychological support) and repeat the ECG after any change.","marks":["Tells her clearly that the ECG is abnormal and shows the finding.","Reads the ECG in order and calculates the QTc with working.","Explains QT prolongation in plain words.","Asks about palpitations, syncope, chest pain and breathlessness.","Checks cardiac and family history, every drug including recent antibiotics, and accidental extra doses.","Links low potassium and calcium, age and sex to the QTc and plans magnesium.","Holds quetiapine and arranges same-day medical assessment.","Plans restart, alternatives, ECG monitoring and relapse watch.","Validates her fear of relapse, checks understanding and safety-nets."],"pitfalls":["Trusting the machine's QTc without checking the QT and rate yourself.","Booking a routine repeat ECG for a QTc over 500 ms.","Blaming the antipsychotic and missing the antibiotic and low potassium.","Using jargon like 'QTc' and 'torsades' without explanation.","Switching to another high-risk drug such as haloperidol.","Ignoring her fear that the voices will return."],"refs":["1. BNF: quetiapine; clarithromycin; indapamide (checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG178 Psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (ECG before and during antipsychotic treatment; checked Nov 2026)","3. MHRA Yellow Card scheme (checked Nov 2026)","4. CredibleMeds QTdrugs list (checked Nov 2026)","5. NHS website: electrocardiogram (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in UK community clinics the psychiatrist owns the ECG result. Do not file it for the GP to find. Phone the medical registrar yourself, and tell the consultant the same day.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-23","n":1223,"prog":"Core: first weeks and on call","icon":"🚑","title":"A routine ECG shows ST elevation: acting on a heart attack in clinic","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 11:40","where":"Community mental health centre outpatient clinic","text":"Dorothy Kemp, 74, has schizophrenia, type 2 diabetes and hypertension. She takes quetiapine MR 400 mg at night, metformin 1 g twice daily and amlodipine 5 mg. Last week's bloods: HbA1c 70 mmol/mol, total cholesterol 7.1 mmol/L, triglycerides 5.8 mmol/L. She came by bus for her routine physical health review. The healthcare assistant hands you her ECG: rate 108, ST elevation in V1–V4 and lead I. She says she just has indigestion."},"task":"Recognise the signs of an acute myocardial infarction on the ECG, check her symptoms, explain the finding in simple terms and arrange emergency transfer.","first60":["Read the ECG before you speak to her: territorial ST elevation plus reciprocal depression is a STEMI until proven otherwise.","See her at once; ask how she feels right now and about the 'indigestion'.","Get someone to call 999 and bring the emergency bag and defibrillator; you stay with her.","Check aspirin allergy and bleeding risk, then give aspirin 300 mg to chew if it's in the bag.","Tell her plainly she is having a heart attack and must not go home."],"ask":["'Where is the pain, what does it feel like and when did it start?'","'Does it spread to your arm, neck, jaw or back?'","'Do you feel sweaty, sick, breathless or faint?'","'Are you allergic to aspirin, or have you had a stomach ulcer or bleeding?'","'Have you taken anything for the pain today?'","'Who should we call for you?'"],"recognise":["STEMI: ST elevation in two or more adjacent leads of one territory, often with reciprocal ST depression opposite and, later, Q waves.","Territories: anterior V1–V4 (septal V1–V2), lateral I, aVL, V5–V6, inferior II, III, aVF.","Widespread concave ST elevation without reciprocal change and with PR depression suggests pericarditis instead.","People with diabetes, older adults and women often present atypically: 'indigestion', breathlessness, nausea or no pain at all.","Time is muscle: primary PCI is the treatment of choice if it can be delivered within 120 minutes of when fibrinolysis could have been given.","Severe mental illness roughly doubles cardiovascular mortality; antipsychotics, smoking, diabetes and missed checks all contribute."],"decide":[{"c":"Call 999 for suspected STEMI, give aspirin 300 mg after checking allergy, stay with her on monitoring and hand over the ECG and history to paramedics.","r":"Fastest route to primary PCI with safe first aid; the ECG travels with her so the cath lab can be pre-alerted.","best":true},{"c":"Repeat the ECG in 30 minutes to see whether the changes persist.","r":"Delay costs heart muscle; with symptoms and territorial ST elevation, act now.","best":false},{"c":"Advise her to go to A&E by bus or taxi and fax the ECG to the GP.","r":"Unsafe: she could arrest on the way and would bypass the direct cath lab pathway that paramedics use.","best":false}],"law":["Consent: she has capacity and agrees to transfer and aspirin; explain clearly so her decision is informed.","If she refused, assess her capacity for this decision under the MCA 2005; if she lacked it, act in her best interests. The MHA cannot be used to treat a heart attack.","Record the time of the ECG, the aspirin dose and time, observations and the handover (GMC Good medical practice)."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I'm worried this isn't indigestion. The tracing shows you're having a heart attack right now. One of the heart's blood vessels is blocked, and we need to get you to a heart hospital straight away."},{"to":"999 call","s":"74-year-old woman in clinic, central chest pain since 8 am into the left arm, sweaty. ECG shows anterior ST elevation. Suspected STEMI. Alert, BP 146/88, sats 96%."},{"to":"Patient (her tablets)","s":"Mainly it's the diabetes, blood pressure and cholesterol. Quetiapine can raise blood fats and sugar too, so we'll review it with you later. Keep taking it for now."}],"write":"11:45 Urgent review in clinic.\nECG 11:35: sinus tachycardia 108, ST elevation V1–V4 (max 4 mm) and I, aVL; reciprocal ST depression II, III, aVF; Q waves V1–V3. Impression: acute anterior STEMI.\nHistory: central burning chest pressure since 08:00, radiating to left arm, clammy; took antacids. No previous cardiac history. No allergies; no peptic ulcer or bleeding.\nObs: BP 146/88, HR 108, SpO2 96% air, RR 18, alert.\nActions: 999 called 11:47 (suspected STEMI). Aspirin 300 mg chewed 11:52. Defibrillator at bedside. No O2 (sats > 94%).\nHandover to paramedics 11:58 with ECG copy, medication list (quetiapine MR 400 mg, metformin 1 g bd, amlodipine 5 mg), recent bloods and mental health history. Conveyed to heart attack centre.\nHusband Alan informed. GP and consultant Dr Hayes informed.","make":["Ask a colleague to call 999 and bring the emergency bag and defibrillator; you stay with her.","Give aspirin 300 mg chewed after checking for allergy and bleeding risk.","Print two copies of the ECG: one for the paramedics, one for the notes.","Hand over to paramedics with SBAR; include her mental health history and medicines.","Phone her husband, the GP and the consultant; complete an incident form if your trust requires it."],"next":"Six weeks later Mrs Kemp returns after a stent, now on aspirin, ticagrelor, atorvastatin 80 mg, ramipril and bisoprolol. She asks whether quetiapine is safe. Review her mental state, check an ECG and lipids, discuss switching to an antipsychotic with less metabolic effect, and liaise with cardiac rehabilitation.","marks":["Recognises anterior STEMI with reciprocal changes on the ECG.","Takes a brief focused chest pain history.","Explains a heart attack in plain words.","Has 999 called with a clear 'suspected STEMI' message.","Checks allergy and gives aspirin 300 mg chewed.","Stays with her, monitors observations and keeps a defibrillator at hand.","Explains she must not go home or drive and why speed matters.","Answers her question about tablets honestly.","Hands over ECG, medicines and history to paramedics and informs family, GP and consultant."],"pitfalls":["Accepting 'indigestion' and filing the ECG for later.","Repeating the ECG instead of calling 999.","Sending her to A&E by taxi or letting her drive.","Giving aspirin without checking for allergy.","Using 'STEMI' and 'PCI' with the patient without explanation.","Telling her to stop her antipsychotic on the spot."],"refs":["1. NICE NG185 Acute coronary syndromes (2020, checked Nov 2026)","2. Resuscitation Council UK: adult in-hospital and community guidance (checked Nov 2026)","3. BNF: aspirin (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: heart attack (checked Nov 2026)","5. NICE CG178 Psychosis and schizophrenia in adults: physical health monitoring (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, a STEMI found outside hospital goes by 999 ambulance straight to a heart attack centre, often bypassing A&E. Do not drive her, send her by taxi, or wait for the GP.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-24","n":1224,"prog":"Core: first weeks and on call","icon":"📈","title":"Presenting an ECG to your consultant, step by step","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 09:30","where":"Community psychosis team office, clozapine clinic morning","text":"You started in the community psychosis team last week. Your consultant, Dr Fiona Gallagher, 58, hands you the annual ECG of Kwame Asante, a 45-year-old man on clozapine 400 mg a day (150 mg mornings, 250 mg at night) for three years. She wants to know you can read ECGs safely, as most of her patients take medicines that affect the heart, and she will ask questions as you go."},"task":"Interpret and present the ECG in a clear, systematic order (details, rate, rhythm, axis, intervals including QTc, ST and T waves) and explain what matters on the ECG for someone on clozapine.","first60":["Start with the patient's name, age, date, reason for the ECG and the calibration.","Use the same order every time: rate, rhythm, axis, P wave and PR, QRS, ST, T, QT and QTc.","Measure, don't guess: count small squares and say the number.","Finish with a one-line summary and a comparison with the last ECG.","Then link it to clozapine: arrhythmia, myocarditis, cardiomyopathy, QTc and ischaemic risk."],"ask":["'Do you want the full system out loud, or just the key findings?'","'Is there a previous ECG to compare with?'","'What dose is he on, and when was it last changed?'","'Any symptoms: chest pain, palpitations, breathlessness, fainting?'","'What does our trust protocol say about troponin and CRP during titration?'"],"recognise":["Rate: QRS complexes in 10 seconds × 6, or 300 ÷ large squares between R waves. Under 60 is bradycardia, over 100 tachycardia.","Normal PR 120–200 ms (3–5 small squares); QRS under 120 ms (3 small squares).","Normal axis: leads I and aVF both positive. Lead I positive with aVF negative suggests left axis deviation if lead II is also negative.","QTc: Bazett is QT ÷ √RR; it overcorrects at fast rates, where Fridericia (cube root) is better.","Clozapine myocarditis is most common in the first 4–8 weeks; ECG may show only tachycardia and non-specific ST/T changes.","Pericarditis: widespread saddle-shaped ST elevation with PR depression and no reciprocal change; an infarct is territorial with reciprocal depression."],"decide":[{"c":"Present in a fixed order with measured values, give a one-line summary, compare with the old ECG, then link to clozapine risks.","r":"Safe, quick to follow and shows you can spot what matters for this drug.","best":true},{"c":"Read out the machine's automated report and say it's normal.","r":"Machine reports misread QT and rhythm often; it shows no system and no clinical judgement.","best":false},{"c":"Jump to the QTc because that's what matters with antipsychotics.","r":"Misses rhythm, ischaemia and conduction problems, which matter as much on clozapine.","best":false}],"law":["GMC Good medical practice: work within your competence and seek advice when unsure; it is safe and expected to say 'I'd check with cardiology'.","NICE CG178: offer an ECG before starting an antipsychotic if the product information requires it, if examination finds cardiovascular risk, if there is a personal history of cardiovascular disease, or if the person is an inpatient.","Clozapine is prescribed only with registration on a monitoring service (blood counts); cardiac monitoring follows local trust protocol."],"say":[{"to":"Consultant (opening)","s":"This is Kwame Asante, 45, recorded today for his annual check on clozapine 400 mg a day. Standard calibration, 25 millimetres a second, 10 millimetres per millivolt."},{"to":"Consultant (summary)","s":"Sinus rhythm at 84, normal axis and intervals, no ST or T changes, QTc about 400. A normal ECG, unchanged from last year."},{"to":"Consultant (myocarditis)","s":"Most likely in the first two months. Look for fever, a persistent fast pulse, chest pain or breathlessness. The ECG may be near normal, so troponin and CRP matter."}],"write":"ECG 10:14 (annual physical health check; clozapine 400 mg/day).\nCalibration standard. Sinus rhythm 84 bpm. Axis normal (~+45°). PR 120 ms. QRS 80 ms. ST isoelectric in all leads; T inversion aVR only. QT 340 ms; QTc 400 ms (Bazett), 380 ms (Fridericia).\nImpression: normal ECG, unchanged from last year (QTc 405 ms).\nNo cardiac symptoms reported at last review.\nReviewed with Dr Gallagher. Next ECG: annual review, or sooner with symptoms or dose change.","make":["File the ECG with your written interpretation, not just the machine report.","Record the QTc and the formula used, and compare with the previous value.","Check the full drug list for other QT-prolonging medicines.","Book the next ECG in his annual physical health review."],"next":"Two months later a new patient, 3 weeks into clozapine titration, has a resting pulse of 118, a temperature of 37.9 °C and mild chest tightness. Her ECG shows sinus tachycardia with T wave flattening. Check troponin and CRP the same day, hold clozapine if they are raised, and arrange urgent medical review.","marks":["States details and calibration before reading.","Uses a fixed order: rate, rhythm, axis, intervals, ST, T, QT.","Measures values in small squares and milliseconds.","Calculates QTc and knows when Bazett misleads.","Gives a one-line summary and compares with the last ECG.","Lists pre-clozapine ECG checks and baseline troponin and CRP.","Describes myocarditis timing, symptoms, ECG and blood tests.","Distinguishes infarct, pericarditis and myocarditis.","Knows when to repeat the ECG and admits uncertainty safely."],"pitfalls":["Reading out the machine report instead of measuring.","Skipping calibration and patient details.","Looking only at the QTc.","Calling a near-normal ECG reassuring in a feverish patient early in clozapine titration.","Confusing pericarditis with an infarct.","Bluffing an answer rather than saying you'd check the protocol."],"refs":["1. NICE CG178 Psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (checked Nov 2026)","2. BNF: clozapine (checked Nov 2026)","3. Clozapine summary of product characteristics, cardiac warnings (checked Nov 2026)","4. Resuscitation Council UK: ECG and arrhythmia recognition resources (checked Nov 2026)","5. Local trust clozapine initiation and monitoring protocol (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK consultants value a fixed system and honesty over speed. Saying 'I'd check our trust protocol' or 'I'd ask cardiology' is seen as safe, not weak.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-25","n":1225,"prog":"Old Age Psychiatry","icon":"🧠","title":"Explaining vascular dementia to her son","src":"","moment":{"when":"Friday 14:00","where":"Older adult community mental health team (memory service)","text":"Margaret Ellison, 78, was referred by her GP after her husband Ron, 80, noticed months of irritability and forgetting, and found her lost in the street three weeks ago. She has type 2 diabetes, hypertension, high cholesterol and a stroke two years ago with residual right arm weakness. Bloods and urine dip are normal. ACE-III 68/100; HADS depression 3/21, anxiety 5/21. MRI: extensive white matter small vessel disease, an old left-sided infarct, generalised volume loss, no bleeds. She still drives locally. Your colleague gave her the diagnosis last week, and she agreed that her son Paul, 50, a hospital porter, could be told. He has asked to see you."},"task":"Explain the diagnosis of vascular dementia to her son, including how the scan and tests support it, the likely course, risk-factor control, support, and driving.","first60":["Confirm that his mother consented to you sharing her information with him.","Ask what he knows already and what is worrying him before explaining anything.","Plan to chunk and check: one idea, then ask if it makes sense.","Have the results to hand: bloods, urine, ACE-III 68, HADS, MRI.","Expect three questions: is it really infection, will I get it, can she have the memory tablets?"],"ask":["'What have you been told so far?'","'What's worrying you most?'","'What changes have you noticed in her yourself?'","'Does that make sense so far?'","'How is your dad coping?'","'What will you tell your dad tonight?'"],"recognise":["Delirium starts over hours to days and fluctuates, with poor attention; this change has built up over months, and bloods and urine are clear.","A low HADS depression score makes depressive pseudodementia unlikely.","Vascular dementia: cognitive decline caused by cerebrovascular disease, often stepwise, with slowed thinking, poor attention and executive problems more than early memory loss.","NICE NG97: do not offer acetylcholinesterase inhibitors or memantine for vascular dementia alone, only where it is mixed with Alzheimer's disease, DLB or PDD.","People with a dementia diagnosis must tell the DVLA; getting lost is a red flag for unsafe driving.","Carers of people with dementia can ask the council for a carer's assessment from the local authority under the Care Act 2014."],"decide":[{"c":"Check consent, ask his views, explain in chunks how tests rule out infection and support vascular dementia, then discuss course, risk factors, tablets, driving and support.","r":"Answers his doubts with evidence, keeps him with you and covers safety.","best":true},{"c":"Start donepezil to show the family something is being done.","r":"NICE advises against cholinesterase inhibitors in pure vascular dementia; it adds side effects without benefit.","best":false},{"c":"Tell him the details are confidential and he must ask his mother.","r":"She has already consented to sharing; refusing would leave the family without the information they need to support her.","best":false}],"law":["Confidentiality: share only with her consent, which she gave; record it (GMC Confidentiality, 2017).","DVLA: a person with dementia must notify the DVLA. If she keeps driving against advice, the GMC allows you to tell the DVLA after trying to persuade her and telling her you will.","Mental Capacity Act 2005: she can make a Lasting Power of Attorney while she has capacity to do so.","Care Act 2014: Ron is able to ask for a carer's assessment from the local authority."],"say":[{"to":"Son","s":"Dementia is an umbrella word for brain changes that steadily affect memory, thinking and daily life. Your mum's type is vascular dementia, caused by damage to the brain's blood vessels."},{"to":"Son (infection)","s":"An infection usually comes on over hours or days and makes people drowsy or muddled from hour to hour. Your mum's change built up over months, and her urine and blood tests were clear."},{"to":"Son (tablets)","s":"The memory tablets help in Alzheimer's but not in vascular dementia alone. The medicines that help her most are the ones protecting her blood vessels."}],"write":"Met son Paul Ellison (50) with Mrs Ellison's documented consent.\nExplained: diagnosis of vascular dementia; basis (history over months, normal bloods and urine excluding delirium, HADS low, ACE-III 68/100, MRI small vessel disease and old left infarct).\nCourse: likely stepwise; risk-factor control discussed.\nAChEIs not indicated (NICE NG97, pure vascular dementia). Letter to GP re BP, HbA1c, statin, antiplatelet adherence.\nInheritance: not directly inherited; advised own NHS Health Check.\nDriving: legal duty to inform DVLA; advised to stop driving pending DVLA decision; to discuss with Mrs Ellison at her review.\nWandering: GPS tracker and Herbert Protocol discussed.\nSupport: CST referral, Alzheimer's Society dementia adviser, carer's assessment for husband, LPA advised.\nLeaflet and team number given. Review in 6 weeks.","make":["Write to the GP: tighten blood pressure, diabetes and cholesterol control, and check adherence to clopidogrel.","Refer for group cognitive stimulation therapy.","Refer to the Alzheimer's Society dementia adviser and request a carer's assessment for her husband.","Discuss driving and DVLA notification with Mrs Ellison at her next review and document her response.","Give a vascular dementia leaflet and the team's contact number."],"next":"Two months later Ron rings: Margaret is still driving to the shops and got lost again yesterday. Speak to her, explain your concern and her legal duty, and tell her you will inform the DVLA if she does not. If she keeps driving, notify the DVLA and write to her to say you have done so.","marks":["Confirms consent to share and asks what he knows and fears.","Explains dementia and vascular dementia in plain words.","Explains how the tests and scan support the diagnosis.","Addresses the infection and depression questions directly.","Describes a stepwise course and risk-factor control.","Answers inheritance accurately and advises on his own risk.","Explains why cholinesterase inhibitors are not used.","Raises driving, the DVLA and wandering safety.","Offers practical support, validates feelings and chunks and checks."],"pitfalls":["Launching into an explanation before finding out what he knows.","Dismissing his infection theory instead of showing the evidence.","Using 'white matter hyperintensities' or 'ACE-III' without explanation.","Offering donepezil for pure vascular dementia.","Forgetting driving.","Giving everything in one long speech without checking understanding."],"refs":["1. NICE NG97 Dementia: assessment, management and support (2018, checked Nov 2026)","2. DVLA: Assessing fitness to drive, dementia and cognitive impairment (checked Nov 2026)","3. GMC Confidentiality: patients' fitness to drive and reporting concerns to the DVLA (checked Nov 2026)","4. Care Act 2014: carers' assessments (checked Nov 2026)","5. NHS website and Alzheimer's Society: vascular dementia (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK families expect a frank explanation and practical next steps. Name the support services (Alzheimer's Society, carer's assessment, LPA, Herbert Protocol); families value these as much as the medical facts.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-26","n":1226,"prog":"Old Age Psychiatry","icon":"🧠","title":"The DaT scan is abnormal: explaining dementia with Lewy bodies to his daughter","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 15:30","where":"Older adult inpatient ward, family room","text":"Mr Kofi Mensah, a 75-year-old man, was moved here seven weeks ago from a medical ward, where he was treated for delirium caused by severe constipation. The confusion never fully lifted. He sees dogs and small children in the bay that are not there. Before admission he swung his walking stick at 'dogs' in the kitchen, broke a glass door and nearly hit his 6-year-old granddaughter. On the medical ward one dose of haloperidol 0.5 mg left him rigid and drowsy for two days. ACE-III 51/100. Last week's DaT scan shows reduced uptake in both striata, consistent with dementia with Lewy bodies. He was told the diagnosis simply yesterday and agreed you can talk to his daughter, Abena, 46, whom he lives with. She asks: 'What is Lewy body? Can he come home?'"},"task":"Explain dementia with Lewy bodies to his daughter: the core features in his own symptoms, what the scan shows, the likely course, and the treatment, including antipsychotic sensitivity and falls.","first60":["Check the notes for his consent to share, and who else in the family needs to hear this.","Read the DaT scan report, the ACE-III breakdown and the drug chart, and note the haloperidol reaction on the medical ward.","List his features against the four core features: fluctuation, visual hallucinations, REM sleep behaviour disorder, parkinsonism.","Check pulse and the admission ECG, because a cholinesterase inhibitor is the likely next step.","Book a quiet room, bring the Alzheimer's Society leaflet on dementia with Lewy bodies, and ask a nurse who knows him to join."],"ask":["'What have you been told up to now, and what do you already know about Lewy bodies?'","'Does he have good and bad days, when he's sharp one hour and muddled or drowsy the next?'","'What does he see, and how does he react when he sees it?'","'Does he shout, punch or kick in his sleep, as if acting out a dream? How long has that been going on?'","'Has he fallen, fainted or seemed stiffer and slower?'","'Which came first: the memory problems or any change in his walking?'","'What worries you most about him coming home?'"],"recognise":["Four core features of dementia with Lewy bodies: fluctuating attention and alertness, recurrent detailed visual hallucinations, REM sleep behaviour disorder, and parkinsonism.","Reduced dopamine transporter uptake in the striata on a DaT scan is an indicative biomarker: with one core feature it supports a diagnosis of probable DLB.","Thinking problems starting before, or within a year of, any movement problem points to DLB rather than Parkinson's disease dementia.","Severe reaction to a small dose of haloperidol is a warning of antipsychotic sensitivity, which can be life-threatening.","Attention and visuospatial skills are hit early in DLB; memory can be relatively spared at first.","Constipation, falls, fainting and low blood pressure on standing are common autonomic features, and often first noticed by family."],"decide":[{"c":"Explain DLB in plain words linked to his symptoms, offer rivastigmine after a pulse check, flag antipsychotic sensitivity on his records, and plan falls and home-safety work before discharge.","r":"Right: an honest, joined-up explanation with the treatment NICE recommends and the single biggest drug danger made visible to every future prescriber.","best":true},{"c":"Start low-dose haloperidol for the hallucinations, as he swung his stick near a child.","r":"Dangerous: he has already reacted badly. Antipsychotics in DLB can cause severe rigidity, falls, sedation and death. Non-drug steps and a cholinesterase inhibitor come first.","best":false},{"c":"Tell her it is 'a bit like Parkinson's' and leave details to the outpatient clinic.","r":"Not enough: she needs the name, what it means for him, the course and the antipsychotic warning now, while discharge is being planned.","best":false}],"law":["Confidentiality (GMC 2017): he agreed to his daughter being told. Record that consent and what was shared.","NICE NG97 (2018): offer donepezil or rivastigmine for mild to moderate DLB; do not offer antipsychotics except in severe distress, and then with great care.","Care Act 2014: Abena is able to ask for a carer's assessment from the local authority.","MCA 2005: his capacity to decide where to live is assessed when discharge is being decided; encourage Lasting Powers of Attorney while he may still be able to make them."],"say":[{"to":"Daughter","s":"I'm afraid the scan, together with everything you've told us, shows that your dad has a type of dementia called dementia with Lewy bodies."},{"to":"Daughter","s":"One thing matters more than almost anything else: people with Lewy bodies can react very badly to antipsychotic medicines. We'll put that warning on all his records, and you should mention it to every doctor he sees."},{"to":"Ward team","s":"Please add 'antipsychotic sensitivity: dementia with Lewy bodies, severe reaction to haloperidol' to his allergies and alerts, and copy it to the GP summary."}],"write":"Family meeting. Daughter Abena Mensah (NOK, lives with patient). Patient consented to information sharing on 6/11.\nCollateral: 9 months of fluctuating confusion; visual hallucinations of dogs and children; acts out dreams for about 5 years; 2 falls this summer; constipation.\nDiscussed: diagnosis of dementia with Lewy bodies (fluctuation, visual hallucinations, probable RBD, mild parkinsonism; DaT scan abnormal). Progressive course, no cure. Rivastigmine planned after pulse/ECG. Antipsychotic sensitivity explained; haloperidol reaction noted.\nActions: alert added to EPR and drug chart; GP letter. OT home visit and physio falls review. Carer's assessment referral. Leaflet given. Teach-back correct.\nFamily meeting with sisters next Wednesday 14:00.\nDr N. Rahman CT2","make":["Ask what she knows first, then give the name early: don't build up for minutes before saying 'dementia'.","Tie each core feature to a story she told you; that is what makes the diagnosis believable.","Explain the scan in one line: it looks at dopamine in the brain's movement centres, and his shows less than normal.","Put the antipsychotic sensitivity alert on the drug chart, the electronic record and the GP letter today.","Pause after the diagnosis and after the prognosis; let her react before you move on.","End with teach-back: 'What will you tell your sisters tonight?'"],"next":"Rivastigmine is started at 1.5 mg twice daily after a normal pulse and ECG. Two weeks later he is more alert in the mornings and the 'dogs' are less frequent. The OT home visit removes a glass panel and rugs, and he goes home with a falls alarm and day centre twice a week.","marks":["Asked what she knew and what worried her before explaining.","Named the diagnosis clearly and early.","Linked fluctuation, hallucinations, sleep and falls to the diagnosis.","Explained the scan in plain words.","Warned clearly about antipsychotic sensitivity and flagged it.","Gave an honest course and a practical falls and home plan."],"pitfalls":["Calling it 'just Parkinson's' or 'a bit of Alzheimer's'.","Giving a lecture on proteins and dopamine without linking it to his life.","Forgetting to flag antipsychotic sensitivity, so the next on-call doctor prescribes haloperidol.","Promising that rivastigmine will stop the illness getting worse."],"refs":["1. NICE NG97 Dementia guideline (2018; checked Nov 2026)","2. McKeith IG et al. DLB Consortium fourth consensus report (Neurology, 2017)","3. BNF: rivastigmine; donepezil (checked Nov 2026)","4. Alzheimer's Society: What is dementia with Lewy bodies? (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, drug sensitivities that are not true allergies still go in the 'allergies and alerts' box of the drug chart and the electronic record, so that on-call doctors see them at 3 am.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-27","n":1227,"prog":"Old Age Psychiatry","icon":"🧠","title":"Falls, hallucinations and memory loss: examining for parkinsonism","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 10:15","where":"Memory clinic, outpatient department","text":"Mr Gerald Whitcombe, a 78-year-old retired joiner, has eight months of worsening memory, three falls since spring, and sees his late brother standing in the garden. His MoCA today is 18/30, with poor attention and clock drawing. He takes amlodipine 5 mg and tamsulosin 400 micrograms. He has never been diagnosed with Parkinson's disease. His wife, June, is in the waiting room. The consultant asks you: 'Examine him for parkinsonism, do a lying and standing BP, and tell me if this looks like Lewy body disease.'"},"task":"Examine him for parkinsonism (tremor, rigidity, bradykinesia, face, posture, gait and postural instability), measure lying and standing blood pressure, and relate the findings to dementia with Lewy bodies.","first60":["Read the referral: which came first, the memory problems or the falls? Any antipsychotic or anti-sickness drug (metoclopramide, prochlorperazine)?","Note BP-lowering drugs: amlodipine and tamsulosin both worsen postural drops.","Set up: a couch he can lie on for 5 minutes, a BP machine, a chair with arms, a clear 6-metre walkway, pen and paper.","Plan to stand behind him for the pull test, with room to catch him and a wall behind you.","Wash hands, introduce yourself, and offer a chaperone and his wife's presence."],"ask":["'Have you noticed any shaking, stiffness or slowness, for example doing up buttons or writing?'","'Tell me about the falls: did you trip, go dizzy, or just go over?'","'Do you feel light-headed when you stand up?'","'Do you act out your dreams, or has June said you shout or kick in your sleep?'","'Any constipation, trouble swallowing, problems passing water, or loss of smell?'","'Is anything painful today, so I can be gentle?'"],"recognise":["Parkinsonism means bradykinesia plus rest tremor, rigidity or both. Bradykinesia must show decrement: movements getting smaller and slower with repetition.","In DLB parkinsonism is often symmetrical and akinetic-rigid; tremor is less prominent than in Parkinson's disease.","A systolic fall of 20 mmHg or more, or diastolic 10 or more, within 3 minutes of standing is orthostatic hypotension: an autonomic feature and a falls cause.","Thinking problems first, or within a year of parkinsonism, with visual hallucinations: probable dementia with Lewy bodies.","Upgoing plantars, stepwise history or lower-body-only parkinsonism suggest vascular parkinsonism; vertical gaze palsy and early backward falls suggest progressive supranuclear palsy.","Drug-induced parkinsonism must be excluded: check for dopamine blockers in the past 6 months."],"decide":[{"c":"Report bradykinesia with decrement, right-sided cogwheel rigidity, reduced arm swing, a positive pull test and a 26 mmHg postural drop: parkinsonism with visual hallucinations and fluctuation, so probable DLB; flag antipsychotic sensitivity and review his BP drugs.","r":"Right: structured findings, a clear link to the core features, and two practical safety actions.","best":true},{"c":"Note 'no tremor, so not Parkinsonian' and move on.","r":"Wrong: tremor is often absent in DLB. Bradykinesia with decrement and rigidity define parkinsonism.","best":false},{"c":"Skip the lying and standing BP because the clinic is busy.","r":"Unsafe: orthostatic hypotension is common in DLB, is a treatable cause of his falls, and changes his prescriptions.","best":false}],"law":["Consent (GMC 2020): explain each part and ask permission before touching; he can stop at any time. Offer a chaperone.","MCA 2005: he can consent to a simple examination if he understands what will happen; most people with mild to moderate dementia can.","NICE NG97: use clinical features to diagnose DLB; consider a DaT scan if the diagnosis is uncertain.","NICE NG249 (2025) on falls: older people who fall should have a multifactorial falls risk assessment, including postural BP and medication review."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I'd like to look at how your muscles and movements are working, and check your blood pressure lying down and then standing. It takes about ten minutes. Tell me if anything hurts and we'll stop."},{"to":"Patient","s":"I'm going to stand behind you and give a quick tug on your shoulders. I'll catch you, I promise. Try not to fall back."},{"to":"Consultant","s":"Bradykinesia with decrement, right more than left cogwheel rigidity, hypomimia, reduced right arm swing, positive pull test and a 26 mmHg postural drop. With hallucinations and fluctuation, I think this is probable DLB."}],"write":"Neurological examination for parkinsonism. 78M, MoCA 18/30. Consent obtained; wife present.\nObservation: hypomimia, reduced blink, soft voice. Intermittent fine rest tremor R hand, settles on movement.\nTone: cogwheel rigidity R wrist, mild L, increased with contralateral activation.\nBradykinesia: finger taps and hand opening slow with decrement, R > L. Micrographia.\nGait: stooped, short steps, reduced R arm swing, turns in 5 steps. Pull test: 3 steps back, would fall if not caught.\nEye movements full, including vertical. Plantars down. Power normal.\nBP: lying 148/82 (HR 70); standing 1 min 130/76, 3 min 122/74 (HR 74). Postural drop 26 mmHg systolic, dizzy.\nHistory: acts out dreams 4 years; constipation.\nImpression: parkinsonism + visual hallucinations + fluctuation + probable RBD: probable DLB. Orthostatic hypotension.\nPlan: discussed with Dr Okoye. Review amlodipine and tamsulosin with GP. Antipsychotic sensitivity alert. Falls clinic and physio. DaT scan only if diagnosis disputed.\nDr L. Fernandes ST4","make":["Watch first: face, blink, voice, tremor at rest with hands on the lap, before you touch him.","Bring out hidden rigidity: ask him to open and close the other hand while you rotate the wrist.","Test bradykinesia with at least 10 finger taps; you are looking for decrement, not just slowness.","Lie him down for 5 minutes, then measure at 1 and 3 minutes standing; ask about dizziness each time.","Stand close and behind him for the pull test, and warn him first.","End by summarising findings to him in plain words and linking them to his falls."],"next":"The consultant agrees probable DLB. The GP stops tamsulosin and switches amlodipine to a lower dose; the postural drop falls to 10 mmHg. He starts donepezil after a normal ECG, is referred to the falls clinic, and his record carries an antipsychotic sensitivity alert.","marks":["Explained the examination and gained consent.","Observed face, blink, speech and rest tremor before touching.","Tested tone with activation and compared both sides.","Tested bradykinesia for decrement.","Examined posture, gait, turning and pull test safely.","Measured lying and standing BP and related all findings to DLB."],"pitfalls":["Ruling out parkinsonism because there is no tremor.","Calling slowness 'bradykinesia' without testing for decrement.","Doing the pull test without warning him or without being ready to catch him.","Measuring standing BP straight away instead of after lying for 5 minutes."],"refs":["1. McKeith IG et al. DLB Consortium fourth consensus report (Neurology, 2017)","2. Postuma RB et al. MDS clinical diagnostic criteria for Parkinson's disease. Movement Disorders 2015;30:1591-1601","3. NICE NG97 Dementia (2018; checked Nov 2026)","4. Royal College of Physicians: Measurement of lying and standing blood pressure, a brief guide for clinical staff (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in UK clinics the doctor routinely performs the lying and standing BP. A postural drop with dizziness goes in the GP letter with a clear request to review named BP-lowering drugs.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-28","n":1228,"prog":"Old Age Psychiatry","icon":"🏠","title":"'You've sedated my brother': explaining BPSD and the antipsychotic","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 11:40","where":"Residential care home, visit by the community older adult team","text":"Mr Leonard Fairbairn, an 84-year-old man with Alzheimer's dementia, has lived in the care home for 18 months. His wife died eight months ago. Over the past six weeks he has hit two carers during washing and pushed a resident. A colleague started risperidone 250 micrograms twice daily three weeks ago. He settled, but this week the hitting is back, he is up at night calling his wife's name, and he is eating little. Staff want the dose doubled. As you arrive his brother, Mr Ronald Fairbairn, 80, stops you in the corridor: 'You've drugged him into a zombie. I read about these pills in the paper. Who agreed to this?'"},"task":"Explain behavioural and psychological symptoms of dementia, the risks and limits of risperidone, and the non-drug plan and review, to his angry brother.","first60":["Find a private room; don't argue in the corridor. Offer him a seat and a drink.","Check the care plan: whether anyone holds a health and welfare LPA, and whether a best-interests decision for risperidone was recorded.","Read the MAR chart, the last two weeks of behaviour notes and any ABC charts, falls, bowel chart, weight and fluid intake.","Note what has changed: grief, new carers, room move, hearing aids, glasses, pain, constipation, infection.","Plan to examine Leonard today before any change to his medicines."],"ask":["'Tell me what you've seen and what worries you most.'","'How does he seem to you now compared with a month ago?'","'What kind of man was he, and what did he enjoy?'","'Does he still talk about his wife? How has he been since she died?'","'Was anyone asked about the risperidone before it started? Do you hold Lasting Power of Attorney for him?'","'Have you noticed anything that sets him off, like washing, noise, or certain times of day?'"],"recognise":["BPSD (agitation, aggression, wandering, calling out, low mood, psychosis) affects most people with dementia at some point and is often a sign of unmet need.","Pain, constipation, infection, delirium, poor sleep, hearing or vision loss, depression and grief are common treatable drivers.","Risperidone is the only antipsychotic licensed in the UK for persistent aggression in moderate to severe Alzheimer's, short term (up to 6 weeks), when there is risk of harm and non-drug measures have failed.","Antipsychotics in dementia raise the risk of stroke and of death; the absolute rise is roughly 1 extra death per 100 people treated over about 12 weeks.","New sedation, falls, stiffness, poor eating or swallowing on risperidone are side effects, not 'settling'.","Calling his late wife's name at night may reflect grief or depression, which needs its own assessment."],"decide":[{"c":"Do not increase risperidone. Examine him today and send bloods, assess pain with PAINAD and give regular paracetamol, start ABC charts and a personalised care plan, set a stop date, and review with the brother in two weeks.","r":"Right: treats likely causes first, follows the licence and NICE, and makes the brother part of the plan.","best":true},{"c":"Increase risperidone to 500 micrograms twice daily as staff request.","r":"Wrong: the trigger hasn't been looked for, side effects may already be present, and more drug means more risk of stroke, falls and death.","best":false},{"c":"Stop risperidone today because the brother is angry.","r":"Not on its own: decisions follow his best interests, not who shouts loudest. Review the dose against side effects and causes, and plan a taper with a stop date.","best":false}],"law":["MCA 2005: if Leonard lacks capacity for this decision, it is made in his best interests, consulting his brother. If his brother holds a health and welfare LPA, he makes the decision.","NICE NG97: offer antipsychotics only if he is at risk of harming himself or others or is severely distressed; discuss benefits and harms, use the lowest dose for the shortest time, and reassess at least every 6 weeks.","Risperidone licence (SmPC): short-term treatment, up to 6 weeks, of persistent aggression in moderate to severe Alzheimer's dementia.","Complaints: the brother can raise concerns with the care home manager or our trust's PALS; tell him how without being defensive."],"say":[{"to":"Brother","s":"You're right to question this. These medicines have real risks in dementia, and they should never be used just to keep someone quiet."},{"to":"Brother","s":"I'm not going to increase the dose today. First I want to find out what's upsetting him: pain, constipation, an infection, or grief for his wife."},{"to":"Care home manager","s":"Please don't give extra risperidone. Start ABC charts for two weeks, a bowel chart, and regular paracetamol from today, and note his response to music during personal care."}],"write":"Care home review. 84M, Alzheimer's dementia (moderate). Risperidone 250 micrograms BD started 3 weeks ago for aggression.\nBrother Ronald (NOK, no LPA) present; concerns about sedation.\nHistory: aggression returned this week, mostly during washing; up at night calling late wife's name; eating less. Bowels not opened for 4 days. Knee pain grimace on standing (PAINAD 5).\nO/E: drowsy but rousable, mild cogwheel rigidity both wrists, no fever, abdomen soft, mildly distended.\nPlan: no dose increase. Regular paracetamol 1 g QDS. Macrogol. Bloods: FBC, U&E, CRP, LFT, TFT, glucose, calcium. No urine dipstick (over 65s). ABC chart 2 weeks. Music and photo of wife during care. Risperidone reduce to 250 micrograms nocte from tomorrow; stop date 28/11 unless risk returns. Best-interests decision recorded with brother.\nReview 2 weeks with brother; GP informed.\nDr A. Mensah ST5","make":["Let him finish. Reflect his anger back in one sentence before any facts.","Agree with what is true: the risks are real and the drug should be short term.","Give two concrete reasons the behaviour may be happening (pain, constipation, grief) and how you'll check each.","State the stop date and the review date out loud.","Ask him what his brother loved, and build it into the care plan: music, photos, routines.","Tell him how to raise a concern formally, without sounding defensive."],"next":"Leonard's bowels open after macrogol and paracetamol eases his knee. Carers play Glenn Miller during washing, and the hitting stops within a week. Risperidone is stopped at the six-week review. Ronald brings photographs of Leonard's wife for his room.","marks":["Let the brother vent and validated his anger.","Explained BPSD as a sign of unmet need, not 'bad behaviour'.","Gave honest risks of risperidone, including stroke and death.","Looked for causes: pain, constipation, infection, grief.","Described a specific non-drug plan.","Set a stop date and review, involving the brother."],"pitfalls":["Becoming defensive or blaming the colleague who prescribed it.","Underplaying the risks to calm him down.","Increasing the dose without looking for a cause.","Using a urine dipstick to diagnose infection in an 84-year-old."],"refs":["1. NICE NG97 Dementia: assessment, management and support (2018; checked Nov 2026)","2. BNF and SmPC: risperidone (checked Nov 2026)","3. MHRA: Antipsychotics: risk of stroke and death in elderly patients with dementia (checked Nov 2026)","4. UKHSA: Diagnosis of urinary tract infections, quick reference tool for primary care (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in UK care homes, next of kin cannot consent for an adult unless they hold a health and welfare Lasting Power of Attorney; otherwise the prescriber makes a best-interests decision after consulting them, and records it.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-29","n":1229,"prog":"Old Age Psychiatry","icon":"🧠","title":"Not dementia yet: explaining mild cognitive impairment to her husband","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 14:00","where":"Memory clinic, outpatient department","text":"Mrs Margaret Holloway, a 68-year-old retired librarian, has ten months of forgetting appointments, names and items on the shopping list. She still manages the house, finances, cooking and driving without problems. Results: ACE-III 81/100 (memory and fluency lowest), HADS anxiety 6/21, depression 2/21, bloods normal (FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT, B12, folate, calcium, HbA1c, CRP), CT head shows only age-related changes. Her mother had Alzheimer's disease. She has given written consent for you to speak with her husband, Mr Peter Holloway, 70, and is at an eye appointment in the next building. She asked him to 'find out if it's Alzheimer's like Mum'."},"task":"Explain mild cognitive impairment to her husband: what the test results show, the chance of progression, what reduces risk, and the follow-up plan.","first60":["Check her written consent to share, and that she wants him told the diagnosis before she is.","Plan to see her too: the diagnosis is hers, and she should hear it from you directly, not only second-hand.","Line up the results: ACE-III with domain scores, HADS, bloods and CT, so you can explain what each one ruled out.","Check her blood pressure, weight, alcohol, smoking, hearing and medicines in the notes for risk-reduction advice.","Have the NHS and Alzheimer's Society information on mild cognitive impairment ready."],"ask":["'What has Margaret asked you to find out today?'","'What have you noticed, and how does she manage day to day: money, cooking, driving, appointments?'","'What worries you most?'","'How has her mood been, and her sleep?'","'How is her hearing? Any problems with her blood pressure or sugar?'","'How much does she walk or exercise, and does she still see friends?'"],"recognise":["Mild cognitive impairment: memory or thinking worse than expected for age, noticed by the person or others and measurable on testing, but everyday function is preserved. Dementia requires decline in daily function.","ACE-III 81 is just below the usual cut-offs (82 to 88), so there is a measurable deficit; normal HADS, bloods and CT make depression, thyroid, B12 and structural causes unlikely.","MCI is not one disease: some people stay stable, some improve, and some develop dementia, most often Alzheimer's. In memory clinic samples roughly 1 in 10 a year progress.","NICE does not recommend cholinesterase inhibitors or memantine for MCI; they do not prevent progression.","Modifiable risk factors include hearing loss, high blood pressure, diabetes, smoking, inactivity, excess alcohol, social isolation and depression.","Late-onset Alzheimer's in a parent raises risk modestly; routine genetic testing is not recommended."],"decide":[{"c":"Explain MCI as 'not dementia', give honest numbers on progression, explain why no medicine is offered, agree risk-reduction steps, and book a repeat assessment in 12 months, with a plan to see Margaret herself.","r":"Right: accurate, honest, practical, and respects that the diagnosis belongs to her.","best":true},{"c":"Tell him it's early Alzheimer's so they can prepare.","r":"Wrong: MCI is not a dementia diagnosis, many never progress, and labelling it Alzheimer's causes harm and is untrue.","best":false},{"c":"Reassure him it's normal ageing and discharge her.","r":"Wrong: her score shows a real deficit, and follow-up is needed to pick up progression early.","best":false}],"law":["Confidentiality (GMC 2017): she has given written consent to share; record it and tell her what you discussed.","NICE NG97: do not offer cholinesterase inhibitors or memantine to people with mild cognitive impairment.","DVLA: MCI with no effect on driving does not usually stop driving, but tell her to report any concerns; check the current DVLA guidance if in doubt.","MCA 2005: a good time to suggest Lasting Powers of Attorney, while she can make them."],"say":[{"to":"Husband","s":"The good news first: this is not dementia. Margaret has what we call mild cognitive impairment. Her memory is a bit worse than we'd expect for her age, but she's still managing everything in her life."},{"to":"Husband","s":"Some people with this stay the same for years, some even improve, and some go on to develop dementia. Roughly one in ten a year in clinics like ours do, which means most don't in any given year."},{"to":"Husband","s":"There's no tablet that helps at this stage, but there are things that genuinely lower the risk: exercise, blood pressure, hearing and staying socially active."}],"write":"Memory clinic. 68F, MCI. Discussion with husband Peter (written consent from patient on file).\nResults explained: ACE-III 81/100 (memory 19/26, fluency 9/14); HADS A6 D2; bloods normal; CT: age-related changes only.\nFunction: independent in all IADLs including finances and driving.\nExplained: MCI, not dementia; progression about 10% per year in clinic samples; some stable or improve. No anti-dementia medication (NICE). Family history of AD discussed; no genetic testing indicated.\nRisk reduction: exercise 150 min/week, BP check with GP, hearing test, alcohol under 14 units, social activity. LPA suggested.\nTeach-back correct. Leaflets given.\nPlan: see patient with husband today; GP letter; repeat ACE-III and review in 12 months, sooner if decline in daily function.\nDr K. Osei CT3","make":["Lead with 'this is not dementia' and then explain what MCI is.","Explain each test by what it ruled out: low mood, thyroid, vitamins, stroke or tumour.","Give progression as a natural frequency, and say that many people never progress.","Say clearly why no tablet: the medicines don't help at this stage.","Make risk reduction concrete and personal: steps, minutes, a hearing test, a GP BP check.","Arrange to see Margaret herself; she owns the diagnosis."],"next":"Margaret joins them after her eye appointment and hears the diagnosis from you directly. She books a hearing test, joins a walking group, and the GP starts treating her borderline blood pressure. At 12 months her ACE-III is 82 and she is still fully independent.","marks":["Checked consent and what he wanted to know.","Explained MCI clearly as not dementia.","Explained the results and what they ruled out.","Gave honest progression figures without false certainty.","Explained why no medication and gave practical risk reduction.","Arranged follow-up and a plan to see her."],"pitfalls":["Calling it 'early Alzheimer's'.","Offering donepezil 'to be safe'.","Giving reassurance that it's 'just age' and discharging.","Forgetting that the patient herself must also be told."],"refs":["1. NICE NG97 Dementia: assessment, management and support (2018; checked Nov 2026)","2. Livingston G et al. Dementia prevention, intervention, and care: 2024 report of the Lancet standing Commission. Lancet 2024","3. NHS website: Mild cognitive impairment; Alzheimer's Society: Mild cognitive impairment (checked Nov 2026)","4. DVLA: Assessing fitness to drive, a guide for medical professionals (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK memory clinics routinely discuss Lasting Powers of Attorney early, even with MCI. It is seen as good practice, not a sign that the doctor expects dementia.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-30","n":1230,"prog":"Old Age Psychiatry","icon":"⚖️","title":"Going home without carers: capacity to refuse a care package","src":"","moment":{"when":"Friday 10:30","where":"Acute medical ward, side room","text":"Mrs Dorothy Ashworth, an 82-year-old widow, came in three weeks ago with a urinary infection that spread to her blood, then caught pneumonia on the ward. She is now medically fit for discharge. She had a fall on the ward without injury. She lives alone in a two-bedroom terrace, walks with a stick, and has osteoarthritis, hypertension, type 2 diabetes and cataracts awaiting surgery. Medicines: amlodipine 10 mg, metformin, atorvastatin. 4AT today 0; MoCA 27/30. The OT recommends carers twice a day for washing, dressing and meals. She refuses. The consultant asks you: 'Please assess her capacity to refuse carers, tell her your conclusion, and help us make discharge as safe as possible.'"},"task":"Assess her capacity to refuse carers after discharge, tell her the outcome, and agree a safe discharge plan either way.","first60":["Be clear on the exact decision: refusing twice-daily carers for washing, dressing and meals after discharge, not 'going home' in general.","Read the OT report and the falls record so you can explain the specific reasons carers were advised.","Check the delirium has cleared: 4AT today, nursing notes overnight, observations.","Note her vision, hearing and best time of day; bring her glasses and make sure the room is quiet.","Start from the presumption that she has capacity; your job is to support her to decide, not to change her mind."],"ask":["'What's your understanding of why the team suggested carers?'","'What would the carers actually do for you?'","'What might happen if you go home without them?'","'How would you manage washing, dressing and meals in the first week?'","'If you fell at home, what would you do?'","'What matters most to you about going home?'","'How's your mood been? Do you ever feel lonely?'"],"recognise":["MCA principles: presume capacity, take all practicable steps to help her decide, and an unwise decision is not proof of incapacity.","Capacity is decision-specific and time-specific. Assess the functional abilities: understand, retain, use or weigh, and communicate. Then consider whether any inability is because of an impairment of mind or brain.","Recent delirium is a possible impairment, but 4AT 0 and MoCA 27/30 suggest it has cleared; she still needs a functional assessment.","A capacitous refusal is respected even if risky; the duty then is to reduce risk with what she will accept.","Falls risk factors to address: poor vision from cataracts, arthritis, postural hypotension (amlodipine 10 mg), recent infection and deconditioning.","Living alone after a long admission brings risks of poor nutrition, self-neglect, loneliness and low mood; screen for depression."],"decide":[{"c":"Conclude she has capacity to refuse carers, tell her so clearly, and agree a plan she accepts: community OT and physio, pendant alarm, neighbours' support, GP review, falls clinic, postural BP and amlodipine review, and an open offer of carers.","r":"Right: respects a capacitous decision and still makes the discharge as safe as possible.","best":true},{"c":"Conclude she lacks capacity because refusing carers after a fall is unwise.","r":"Wrong: an unwise decision is not evidence of incapacity. She understood, retained and weighed the risks.","best":false},{"c":"Delay discharge until she agrees to carers.","r":"Wrong: holding a capacitous adult in hospital to force a care package is unlawful and harms her (deconditioning, infection risk).","best":false}],"law":["MCA 2005 s1: presume capacity; all practicable steps to support the decision; unwise decisions do not mean incapacity.","MCA 2005 ss2–3: inability to understand, retain, use or weigh, or communicate information relevant to the decision, because of an impairment of mind or brain. Record the decision assessed and how each part was met.","If she lacked capacity: best-interests decision under s4, with her wishes, feelings and values central, consulting those close to her, and the least restrictive option.","Care Act 2014: she can refuse care and support, and can ask for an assessment again at any time; reablement is free for up to six weeks."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I'm not here to make you have carers. I'm here to make sure you've got all the information, and to understand how you've come to your decision."},{"to":"Patient","s":"You've clearly understood the risks and thought them through. It's your decision to make, and I'll tell the team that."},{"to":"Consultant","s":"She has capacity to refuse twice-daily carers. She understood, retained and weighed the risks, including falls and meals, and told me her reasons clearly. She accepts community OT, physio, a pendant alarm and GP follow-up."}],"write":"Capacity assessment. Decision: whether to accept twice-daily carers (washing, dressing, meals) on discharge.\nMs Dorothy Ashworth, 82F. Delirium resolved: 4AT 0, MoCA 27/30. Glasses on, quiet room, morning.\nUnderstand: explained in own words what carers would do and why (fall on ward, slow mobility, recent illness).\nRetain: repeated the information accurately 20 minutes later.\nUse/weigh: acknowledged falls risk and difficulty with washing; weighed against independence, infection risk in hospital, neighbours' help. Described what she would do after a fall.\nCommunicate: clear and consistent.\nConclusion: has capacity to refuse carers. Unwise to some, but capacitous. Conclusion explained to her.\nRisk screen: no depressive symptoms; occasional loneliness. Dizzy on standing.\nPlan agreed: community OT home visit within a week; community physio; pendant alarm; neighbours to help with shopping and meals; Age UK befriending referral; lying/standing BP before discharge and amlodipine review; GP review in 2 weeks; falls clinic; carers can be requested at any time via adult social care.\nDr P. Nwosu CT2","make":["Name the decision at the start, out loud, in one sentence.","Explain the information first, then test each functional ability with open questions.","Test 'use or weigh' with 'what might happen if...' and 'how would you manage...', not 'do you understand?'","Check retention by asking her to repeat it back later in the conversation.","Tell her your conclusion plainly and that the decision is hers.","Build the safety plan from what she will accept, and leave the door open for carers."],"next":"Mrs Ashworth goes home the next day with a pendant alarm. Her lying and standing BP showed a 22 mmHg drop, so amlodipine is reduced. The community OT fits a perching stool and rails. Three weeks later she asks for a carer twice a week for showers.","marks":["Defined the specific decision.","Presumed capacity and supported her to decide.","Assessed understanding, retention, using and weighing, and communication.","Screened for falls causes, mood and isolation.","Told her the conclusion clearly.","Agreed a safe plan she accepted and documented it."],"pitfalls":["Treating an unwise decision as proof of incapacity.","Assessing capacity for 'going home' rather than for 'refusing carers'.","Asking only closed questions such as 'Do you understand?'","Concluding capacity and then walking away without a safety plan."],"refs":["1. Mental Capacity Act 2005, sections 1–4, and Code of Practice (checked Nov 2026)","2. A Local Authority v JB [2021] UKSC 52 (structure of the capacity test)","3. NICE NG249 Falls: assessment and prevention in older people (2025; checked Nov 2026)","4. Care Act 2014 statutory guidance: intermediate care and reablement (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK an adult with capacity can refuse any care, however risky. Your duty shifts from persuading to reducing risk, and the capacity note must name the decision and how each part of the test was met.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-31","n":1231,"prog":"Core: first weeks and on call","icon":"⚖","title":"Rent arrears: can he manage his own money?","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 14:00, community clinic","where":"Community mental health team base, outpatient room","text":"Liam Doyle, a 34-year-old man with paranoid schizophrenia, has been stable on quetiapine for three years and lives alone in a housing association flat. He is five months behind with his rent and has been served a notice seeking possession. His housing officer has asked the team whether he can manage his own money, and his care coordinator has booked him in with you."},"task":"Assess Liam's capacity to make decisions about managing his money, tell him the outcome in plain words, and agree practical support that keeps him in control.","first60":["Read the notes first: last relapse, current symptoms, quetiapine dose, and any history of money being taken from him.","Pin down the decision you are testing: managing his day-to-day income, rent and bills. Not 'capacity' in general.","Presume capacity and say so: 'This is about one decision, and the starting point is that you can make it.'","Have the facts to check his answers against: his income, rent and the arrears figure in the housing officer's letter.","Plan for both outcomes: support he chooses if he has capacity; best interests and the least restrictive option if he does not."],"ask":["'How have you been in yourself? Any voices, or feeling people are against you?'","'Do you feel the housing association or anyone else is targeting you over money?'","'What comes in each month, and from where?'","'What has to go out: rent, gas, electric, phone, food?'","'How much do you owe now, and how did it build up?'","'If you paid for a £4.85 meal deal with a £10 note, what change would you get?'","'If the rent isn't paid, where could that lead?'","'What's good and bad about handling it all yourself, compared with getting help?'","'Has anyone been borrowing money from you or asking you for it?'"],"recognise":["Capacity is decision-specific and time-specific: running day-to-day money is a different decision from signing a tenancy or making a big one-off purchase.","Unwise is not incapacity: missing rent through disorganisation, with good understanding, does not show he lacks capacity.","Illness matters only if it stops him understanding, retaining, using or weighing, or communicating. Look for paranoid ideas about the landlord, grandiose beliefs about money, or voices driving spending.","Negative symptoms, sedation and poor planning cause missed bills with capacity intact; the answer is practical help, not a capacity label.","Financial exploitation by 'friends', gambling, drink and drugs can all look like incapacity; ask directly.","If capacity could fluctuate with relapse, record today's finding and plan to reassess if he becomes unwell."],"decide":[{"c":"Test all four abilities against his real figures, tell him he has capacity, then offer choices: rent paid direct by Universal Credit, debt advice, a support worker, banking app reminders.","r":"Follows the MCA principles and fixes the real problem, the rent, while he stays in control.","best":true},{"c":"Recommend an appointee or deputy because he has schizophrenia and is in arrears.","r":"Unlawful reasoning: a diagnosis and a poor outcome are not evidence of incapacity, and it strips his independence for no reason.","best":false},{"c":"Tell the housing officer he 'has capacity' and close the referral.","r":"Half the job: he still faces eviction. Agree help he wants, and share only what he consents to.","best":false}],"law":["MCA 2005 s1: presume capacity; take all practicable steps to help him decide; an unwise decision is not proof of incapacity; act in best interests and choose the least restrictive option if he lacks it.","MCA s2 and s3: he lacks capacity only if he cannot understand, retain, use or weigh the information, or communicate a decision, because of an impairment or disturbance of mind or brain. Test the abilities first, then the causal link (A Local Authority v JB [2021] UKSC 52).","If he lacked capacity: a DWP appointee can manage benefits; a Court of Protection property and affairs deputy covers wider finances. A Lasting Power of Attorney must be set up while he still has capacity.","Confidentiality: share the outcome with the housing officer only with his consent (GMC confidentiality guidance)."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"You understood your money, held on to the facts, weighed up both sides and told me your choice clearly. So you can make your own decisions about your money. Missing the rent was about organisation, not your illness."},{"to":"Patient","s":"The choice is yours. Some people find it easier to have the rent paid straight to the landlord, so it can't slip. Would that help you?"},{"to":"Housing officer (with his consent)","s":"Liam has capacity to manage his finances. He has asked Universal Credit to pay his housing costs to you directly and is seeing a debt adviser this week. His care coordinator is your contact."}],"write":"14:40 Capacity assessment: managing day-to-day finances. 34M, paranoid schizophrenia, quetiapine MR 400 mg nocte, adherent.\nMental state: euthymic; no hallucinations or paranoid ideas about landlord or money elicited.\nUnderstand: income UC approx £1,140/month incl housing element; rent £520; bills approx £150; arrears approx £2,600.\nRetain: repeated arrears and eviction risk at end of interview.\nUse/weigh: independence vs 'nobody catches a missed payment'; compared self-payment, managed payment and support worker.\nCommunicate: clear choice, consistent when re-asked.\nOutcome: has capacity for this decision. Arrears due to disorganisation, not mental disorder. Told him.\nPlan (agreed): UC managed payment of rent to landlord; Citizens Advice debt appointment; support worker 4 weeks for direct debits. Consent to share outcome and plan (not health details) with housing officer. Reassess if relapse. Review 1 month.\nDr S. Mensah CT2","make":["Name the decision in one line before you start: 'managing his income, rent and bills'.","Mental state first, briefly: mood, voices, paranoid beliefs about the landlord, the police or his finances; insight; adherence.","Understand: income sources and amounts, fixed outgoings, arrears, what a bank account and direct debit do. Check his figures against the housing letter.","Basic money skills: one simple change sum and one budget question ('After the rent, what's left for the month?').","Use and weigh: consequences of not paying, and the pros and cons of managing alone versus with help.","Retain: ask him to tell back the key figures and risks near the end. Communicate: his decision in his own words, re-asked once.","Then the outcome, his choice of support, consent to share, and a review date."],"next":"Liam asks Universal Credit through his online journal to pay the rent direct and books a Citizens Advice appointment. A month later the support worker reports two rent payments made and a repayment plan agreed; the housing association pauses court action.","marks":["Named the specific decision and presumed capacity.","Checked whether his illness was touching his money decisions.","Tested all four abilities using his real figures.","Did not equate diagnosis or an unwise outcome with incapacity.","Told him the outcome clearly and kindly.","Agreed support he chose and sought consent before sharing."],"pitfalls":["Using a general memory test instead of testing the decision itself.","Concluding incapacity from the arrears alone.","Finding capacity and then offering nothing practical.","Forgetting to ask about exploitation, gambling or substances."],"refs":["1. Mental Capacity Act 2005, sections 1 to 4, and the MCA Code of Practice (2007; checked Nov 2026)","2. A Local Authority v JB [2021] UKSC 52 (checked Nov 2026)","3. GMC. Confidentiality: good practice in handling patient information (2017; checked Nov 2026)","4. GOV.UK. Universal Credit: alternative payment arrangements, including managed payments to landlords (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, money problems in mental illness are usually solved with support, not by taking control. Appointees and deputies are only for people who lack capacity. Know your local welfare rights and debt advice services.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-32","n":1232,"prog":"Old Age Psychiatry","icon":"🏠","title":"'My insides are rotten': psychotic depression at home","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 11:30, urgent home visit","where":"Her terraced house; older adult community mental health team","text":"The GP asks for a same-day visit to Mrs Edna Pryce, a 78-year-old widow living alone. Her daughter Susan says she has barely eaten for two weeks and has taken only sips of tea for three days. She keeps saying her 'insides are rotten'. The GP started mirtazapine 15 mg three weeks ago. Her husband Gerald died of stomach cancer ten months ago."},"task":"Take a focused history and mental state, test how firmly the 'rotten insides' belief is held, assess risk including food and fluid intake, and decide how urgently she needs treatment.","first60":["Before you go in: GP summary, recent bloods, medicines, allergies, and whether anyone is with her.","Follow lone-working rules or go with a colleague; tell the base when you arrive and when you leave.","Look as you enter: dry lips, weight loss, untouched food, a cold house, unopened post, smell of urine.","Sit at her level, introduce yourself slowly, and use short, simple questions; she may answer in single words.","Make intake an early risk question, not an afterthought: 'What have you had to eat and drink since Monday?'"],"ask":["'How have your spirits been since Gerald died? Is any part of the day worse?'","'What have you eaten and drunk in the last three days? When did you last pass water?'","'You said your insides are rotten. Tell me more. How do you know?'","'How sure are you? Could anything else explain how your body feels?'","'Do you feel you've done something wrong, or that you deserve this?'","'Do you hear voices when no one's there, or smell things others can't?'","'Do you feel you'd be better off dead? Have you thought of ending your life?'","'Have you had depression before? What helped?'","'Who is with you each day? Would you accept treatment, or come into hospital?'"],"recognise":["Severe depression with psychotic features: pervasive low mood, anhedonia, slowing, early waking, weight loss, plus mood-congruent delusions of guilt, poverty or bodily decay.","Nihilistic (Cotard-type) delusions: belief that organs are rotting, the body is dead or does not exist, held with full conviction despite evidence.","Separate delusion from overvalued idea or worry: test conviction, the evidence she relies on, and her response to a gentle alternative.","Refusing food and fluid because of a delusion is a medical emergency in an older adult: dehydration, kidney injury, falls, delirium and pressure damage can develop within days.","Rule out physical causes of weight loss and confusion: cancer (feared after her husband's death), hypercalcaemia, thyroid disease, infection, delirium.","Suicide risk is high in older adults with psychotic depression, especially widowed people living alone; passive death wishes count."],"decide":[{"c":"Act today: check pulse, lying and standing BP and signs of dehydration at home; if she is dry, confused or has drunk almost nothing for over 24 hours, arrange acute medical admission first for bloods and fluids, then transfer to the older adult ward; use the MHA only if she refuses and the criteria are met.","r":"Treats the immediate threat to life (intake) and the depression in the right order and the right place.","best":true},{"c":"Increase mirtazapine to 30 mg and review in two weeks.","r":"Far too slow: she will be dangerously dehydrated long before an antidepressant can work.","best":false},{"c":"Refer to the crisis team for daily visits and leave her at home.","r":"Visits cannot make her drink; psychotic depression with almost no intake needs a hospital bed and may need ECT.","best":false}],"law":["If she agrees and has capacity to agree, admit informally. If she lacks capacity to consent to admission, informal admission is not the safe route for a deprivation of liberty.","If she refuses and her health is at serious risk, ask the AMHP for an MHA assessment today; Section 2 (up to 28 days) is usual for assessment and treatment.","On the medical ward, treatment of dehydration needs her consent or, if she lacks capacity for it, a best interests decision under the MCA.","ECT for a detained patient needs her capacitous consent or, if she lacks capacity, a SOAD certificate (Section 58A); in an emergency, Section 62."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I can see how frightened you are, and I'm not going to argue with you. I do think you're very unwell with depression, and it's making your body feel wrong. It can be treated."},{"to":"Daughter","s":"Your mum has a severe depression with beliefs that aren't true but feel completely real to her. Because she's hardly drinking, she needs hospital care today."},{"to":"Consultant","s":"78-year-old widow, severe depression with nihilistic delusions, sips only for three days, dry, pulse 104, 25 mmHg postural drop. Passive death wish, no plan. She agrees to go: I'd like same-day medical admission for fluids and bloods, then our ward."}],"write":"11:30 Urgent home visit with CPN. 78F, widow (husband died 10/12 ago, gastric cancer). Mirtazapine 15 mg nocte for 3/52.\nHx: low mood 6/12, much worse 3/52; anhedonia; wakes 03:00; mornings worst; marked weight loss; sips of tea only for 72 h.\nMSE: thin, dry lips; slowed; quiet, sparse speech; low, tearful affect. Fixed belief her insides are rotting and she is dying, 'smells it' on her breath; 100% certain, not shaken by an alternative. Guilt: 'a burden'. Denies voices. Oriented in time; attention reduced (MOYB slow). Partial insight.\nObs: HR 104 reg, BP 128/70 lying, 103/66 standing, T 36.6.\nRisk: passive death wish, no plan, no stockpile; dehydration, falls, self-neglect; lives alone. No risk to others.\nImp: severe depressive episode with psychotic symptoms (nihilistic delusions); clinical dehydration.\nPlan: agrees to admission. Same-day medical admission via GP and medical registrar: U&E, FBC, glucose, Ca, LFT, TFT, B12, folate, CRP, ECG. Then older adult ward; ECT to be discussed. Daughter informed with consent and staying until transfer. Consultant aware.\nDr R. Khan CT3","make":["Intake: count food and drinks for each of the last three days; ask about urine colour, last void, dizziness on standing and falls.","Bedside checks: dry mouth and tongue, pulse, lying and standing BP, temperature, new confusion.","Core symptoms: mood, anhedonia, energy; then sleep, appetite, weight, concentration, diurnal variation, slowing.","Psychotic features: content (rotting, dead, guilt, poverty), the evidence, certainty, effect on eating; then voices and smells.","Delirium screen: orientation and attention (months backwards); ask the daughter about fluctuation.","Risk: passive and active suicidal thoughts, plans, stockpiled tablets, self-neglect, others, safeguarding.","Insight and willingness: 'Do you think you might be unwell? Would you accept treatment or come into hospital?'"],"next":"Mrs Pryce agrees to go with Susan. On the medical ward her sodium and urea are raised and she has IV fluids. Two days later she moves to the older adult ward, where the team discusses ECT with her and her family.","marks":["Asked in detail about food and fluid intake and urine output.","Elicited core, biological and cognitive symptoms of depression.","Characterised the nihilistic belief and tested its certainty.","Asked directly about suicidal thoughts, plans and stockpiled tablets.","Considered physical causes and delirium.","Set same-day action by her physical risk."],"pitfalls":["Leaving fluid intake to the end, or never asking.","Arguing with the delusion.","Calling it grief and planning routine follow-up.","Accepting 'no plans' and missing a strong passive death wish."],"refs":["1. NICE NG222 (adult depression, 2022); checked Nov 2026","2. NICE TA59 (2003), on electroconvulsive therapy; checked Nov 2026","3. NICE CG103 Delirium: prevention, diagnosis and management in hospital and long-term care (2010, updated 2023; checked Nov 2026)","4. Department of Health. Mental Health Act 1983 Code of Practice (2015; checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in older adults, severe depression often shows as not eating, slowing or 'confusion' rather than sadness. Ask about drinks early, check a lying and standing BP, and never wait for an antidepressant to work when intake has stopped.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-33","n":1233,"prog":"Old Age Psychiatry","icon":"🧠","title":"Dressing and drawing problems: parietal lobe testing","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 15:00, ward round follow-up","where":"Older adult inpatient ward, side room","text":"Mrs Iris Bennett, a 75-year-old right-handed retired bookkeeper, was admitted two weeks ago with vascular dementia and not coping at home. Nurses say she puts her cardigan on upside down, can't fill in her menu card and got muddled with change at the ward shop. Her CT report is back but she has not been told it yet. Your consultant asks you to test her parietal lobe function."},"task":"Carry out a bedside parietal lobe assessment (neglect, apraxia, agnosia, Gerstmann features, constructional and sensory tests) and say what the findings mean.","first60":["Hand hygiene, introduce yourself, explain the purpose in plain words, get consent and offer a chaperone.","Check handedness, glasses, hearing, and any weakness or numbness: you cannot call it apraxia or agnosia if she can't see, hear, move or feel.","Have ready: pen, plain paper, a card with a short command, a coin, a key, a letter and envelope, a cardigan, a family photo.","Tell her each test takes seconds and some are meant to be hard, so failing one is expected.","Plan the order: fields and neglect, Gerstmann features, praxis, drawing, touch with eyes closed, then awareness and recognition."],"ask":["'Which hand do you write with?'","'Look at my nose. Tell me when you see my finger move.' Then both hands at once.","'Show me your ring finger on your left hand.' 'Touch your right ear with your left hand.'","'What's 100 take away 7? Keep going.' 'Write me a sentence about the weather.'","'Show me how you'd wave goodbye, brush your teeth, use a key.'","'Turn this cardigan the right way out and put it on.'","'Copy these overlapping shapes and this cube. Draw a clock with hands at ten past five.'","Eyes closed: 'What's in your hand? What number am I drawing? One point or two?'","'Do you get lost on the ward? Any problems with your arms or legs? Who's in this photo?'"],"recognise":["Dominant (usually left) parietal: Gerstmann features (dyscalculia, agraphia, finger agnosia, left-right confusion) and ideomotor apraxia; all four together is rare.","Non-dominant (usually right) parietal: neglect of the left side, dressing apraxia, anosognosia, topographical disorientation.","Constructional apraxia occurs with either side: left lesions tend to simplify drawings; right lesions disorganise them spatially and drop the left side.","Either side: cortical sensory loss (astereognosis, agraphaesthesia, poor two-point discrimination, sensory inattention) with intact basic touch.","Visual field: an inferior quadrantanopia points to the parietal optic radiation; check fields before calling neglect.","Body-part-as-object (using a finger as a toothbrush) after a prompt suggests ideomotor apraxia."],"decide":[{"c":"Test all domains briefly in a fixed order, then localise: here, dyscalculia, agraphia and ideomotor apraxia (left parietal) plus dressing apraxia and getting lost (right parietal), with no neglect or sensory loss: bilateral parietal involvement, more on the left, fitting vascular dementia.","r":"A complete, reasoned picture the team can use, and one that matches her CT.","best":true},{"c":"Spend most of the time on the clock and sums, then stop.","r":"Misses praxis, sensory and awareness tests, so you cannot say which side is involved.","best":false},{"c":"Record 'parietal signs present' without detail.","r":"Unhelpful: OT, nurses and the consultant need to know which tasks fail and why.","best":false}],"law":["Consent: she can agree to or decline bedside testing; if she cannot understand the purpose, proceed in her best interests (MCA 2005) only if it is needed for her care.","Results disclosure: she has a right to know her CT result. If asked, don't dodge or guess; arrange for the doctor who knows the result to tell her soon.","Her cognitive profile matters later for capacity decisions (money, discharge destination): document it clearly, but capacity is always decision-specific."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Some of these tests are meant to be hard. Struggling with one tells us which part of the brain needs help; it isn't a pass or fail."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Numbers, writing, dressing and copying shapes are harder for you now. That's useful for us to know, because we can make each of them easier."},{"to":"Consultant","s":"Right-handed, fields full, no neglect. Dyscalculia and agraphia, but finger naming and left-right intact: partial Gerstmann. Ideomotor apraxia. Dressing and constructional apraxia, gets lost on the ward. Cortical sensation intact. Bilateral parietal dysfunction, worse on the left, fits her vascular disease."}],"write":"15:20 Parietal lobe bedside testing. 75F, right-handed, vascular dementia. Consent; HCA chaperone. Glasses on, hearing adequate, no limb weakness.\nFields: full to confrontation each eye; no visual inattention.\nDominant: finger naming and L-R orientation intact. Serial 7s: 93 then unable (dyscalculia). Writing: 'It is', unable to continue (agraphia).\nPraxis: waves correctly; body-part-as-object for toothbrush, persists after prompt (ideomotor apraxia). Letter sequence intact. Dressing apraxia with inside-out cardigan.\nConstructional: pentagons not overlapping, cube flattened; clock numbers complete both sides, uneven spacing, hands misplaced.\nSensory (eyes closed): stereognosis, graphaesthesia, 2-point discrimination, position sense, double touch all intact.\nNo anosognosia for memory; topographical disorientation (cannot find toilet without sign). Recognises family photo.\nImp: bilateral parietal dysfunction, left more than right; partial Gerstmann; consistent with vascular dementia.\nPlan: OT functional assessment (dressing, money, menu); dressing prompts in care plan; consultant to discuss CT with patient tomorrow.\nDr T. Afolabi CT1","make":["Fields: each eye covered in turn, four quadrants, then both hands moving at once for visual inattention.","Gerstmann: name or show fingers; crossed left-right command; serial 7s or a written sum; write a sentence.","Ideomotor praxis: wave, salute, brush teeth, use a key; copy two hand postures. Ideational: a three-step sequence (fold, insert, seal).","Dressing: cardigan inside out; watch which sleeve she finds.","Constructional: overlapping pentagons, a cube, a clock with numbers and hands; look for missing left-sided detail.","Eyes closed: coin and key in each hand; draw a number on each palm; one or two points; finger up or down; touch both hands at once.","Then awareness and recognition: anosognosia, getting lost, faces (a family photo or well-known face)."],"next":"The OT finds she can dress with clothes laid out in order and a verbal prompt. The nurses read the menu to her and she points to her choice. Next day Dr Shah explains her CT: small vessel disease and an old left parietal stroke, which fits what you found.","marks":["Checked handedness, vision, hearing and weakness before testing.","Tested fields and visual inattention before judging neglect.","Tested all four Gerstmann features.","Tested ideomotor, ideational, dressing and constructional praxis.","Tested cortical sensation with eyes closed.","Localised the findings and turned them into practical help."],"pitfalls":["Spending half the time on the clock drawing.","Calling neglect without checking the fields.","Calling apraxia in someone with a weak arm or who has not understood the instruction.","Telling her scan results you have not read or are not responsible for."],"refs":["1. Tabi and Husain, bedside testing of parietal function, Practical Neurology 2023;23:404-407; checked Nov 2026","2. Hodges JR. Cognitive Assessment for Clinicians, 3rd ed. Oxford University Press, 2017 (checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE NG97 (2018), on dementia care and support; checked Nov 2026"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: parietal testing is quick when you carry the kit. Keep a coin, a key, a pen, a short written command and plain paper in your pocket, and always ask about handedness first.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-s-34","n":1234,"prog":"Old Age Psychiatry","icon":"🧠","title":"After a first seizure: temporal lobe testing","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 14:30, liaison referral","where":"Elderly care ward, acute hospital","text":"Ms Pauline Hartley, a 68-year-old right-handed retired primary teacher, was admitted this morning after her first generalised seizure at home. She is now alert and frightened. Her CT head is pending formal report. The medical team asks liaison psychiatry to assess her memory and language before her MRI, as her sister says she has been 'vague' for months."},"task":"Carry out a bedside temporal lobe assessment (memory, language and comprehension, naming, visual fields, recognition and seizure phenomena) and say what the findings mean.","first60":["Check the notes: time of the seizure, how long she was drowsy afterwards, glucose, sodium, calcium, any sedation given, and the CT.","Hand hygiene, introduce yourself, explain the purpose, get consent, offer a chaperone.","Check glasses, hearing, handedness and first language; record that she is a few hours after a seizure.","Give the name and address early so you can test delayed recall at the end.","Plan the order: seizure experiences, memory (registration, recent, remote, semantic), language, naming, reading and writing, fields and faces, mood and psychosis, recall."],"ask":["'Before the fit, any odd smells or tastes, a rising feeling in your stomach, or a feeling you'd lived a moment before?'","'Repeat this name and address; I'll ask again later.'","'What did you eat this morning? How did you get here?'","'Where did you grow up? What was your first school called?'","'Who was the first woman UK Prime Minister? Name a landmark in London.' 'Name as many animals as you can in a minute.'","'Point to the ceiling, then the door, then touch your nose.' 'Say after me: ...'","'What's this called? And this part?' (watch, strap, buckle; pen, lid, clip)","'Read this and do what it says.' 'Write me a sentence.'","'Look at my nose; tell me when my finger moves, upper and lower on each side.'"],"recognise":["Dominant (usually left) temporal: verbal memory loss (poor recall of an address), word-finding and naming difficulty for less common words, impaired comprehension (Wernicke-type), with fluent speech.","Non-dominant (usually right) temporal: poor visual memory, prosopagnosia, impaired recognition of tunes and emotional tone.","Either side: superior quadrantanopia ('pie in the sky') from Meyer's loop, on the side opposite the lesion.","Reading and writing failure with fluent speech suggests spread to the temporoparietal junction (angular gyrus).","Focal aware seizures from the temporal lobe: déjà vu, olfactory or gustatory auras, rising epigastric feeling, fear; impaired awareness with staring and lip-smacking or picking.","Postictal confusion can last hours: findings soon after a seizure must be repeated before you call them fixed."],"decide":[{"c":"Test every temporal domain, localise the pattern (here, left temporal with temporoparietal spread and a right upper field defect), flag the earlier focal seizures, and advise repeat testing after recovery plus MRI and EEG.","r":"Answers the referral, gives the team a clear localisation and avoids over-reading a postictal state.","best":true},{"c":"Do a quick memory screen only and report 'cognitive impairment, likely dementia'.","r":"Misses the focal pattern and the months of temporal seizures, and labels her too early.","best":false},{"c":"Wait until she is fully recovered before doing anything.","r":"A baseline now is useful; just say clearly that it will be repeated.","best":false}],"law":["Consent: she can decline. If she lacks capacity to agree because of postictal confusion, wait unless testing now is needed for her care (MCA 2005, best interests).","Driving: after a seizure she must stop driving and inform the DVLA; the doctor should advise this clearly and document it. If she keeps driving against advice, GMC guidance allows disclosure to the DVLA after telling her.","First seizure: NICE NG217 advises specialist assessment within two weeks."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Some of these questions are meant to be harder. Struggling with one helps us find which part of the brain is affected; it isn't a test you pass or fail."},{"to":"Patient","s":"You had trouble holding new information, finding some words, reading and writing, and seeing to the upper right. That points to the left side of the brain. We'll repeat these when you're less tired."},{"to":"Medical registrar","s":"Right-handed, five hours postictal. Poor delayed verbal recall, naming difficulty for low-frequency words, alexia and agraphia, right upper quadrantanopia, with months of déjà vu and olfactory auras. Suggests left temporal focus with temporoparietal involvement. Suggest MRI and EEG; I'll repeat testing in 48 hours."}],"write":"15:00 Liaison: temporal lobe bedside testing. 68F, right-handed, first GTCS 06:30 today; alert, mildly slowed. Consent; HCA chaperone. Glasses on.\nSeizure history: several months of brief burning-rubber smells and deja vu; one witnessed episode of staring and sleeve picking with amnesia.\nMemory: registration of 7-item address intact; recent memory patchy; remote autobiographical and semantic memory intact; animal fluency 9/min (low); delayed recall 0/7 free, 2/7 with cues.\nLanguage: fluent, follows 3-step command, repetition intact; naming of low-frequency items impaired (buckle); alexia ('close your eyes' not read) and agraphia.\nFields: right superior quadrantanopia both eyes. Recognises famous face.\nMood: anxious, low about events; no psychotic symptoms.\nImp: left temporal dysfunction with temporoparietal involvement; probable focal impaired-awareness seizures preceding today's GTCS. Postictal effects possible.\nPlan: discussed with medical registrar; MRI brain and EEG; first seizure clinic; repeat cognitive testing in 48 h; advised not to drive and to inform DVLA.\nDr C. Nwosu CT2","make":["Seizure experiences first: auras (smell, taste, rising stomach feeling, déjà vu, fear), lost time, automatisms seen by others.","Registration: a seven-part name and address, repeated back until correct (up to three tries).","Recent memory (breakfast, how she arrived), remote (childhood, school), semantic (famous facts, landmarks), category fluency (animals in a minute).","Language: spontaneous speech, a three-step command, repetition of a sentence, naming of common then less common parts (watch, strap, buckle).","Reading a written command and writing a sentence of her own.","Visual fields in four quadrants each eye; face recognition with a photo of a famous face.","Mood and psychotic symptoms briefly, then delayed recall, free first and then with cues."],"next":"Her MRI shows a small left temporal lesion and the EEG shows left temporal sharp waves. Neurology starts an antiseizure medicine. Two days later your repeat testing still shows poor verbal recall and naming, but her reading has improved: some of the first findings were postictal.","marks":["Asked about focal seizure experiences before the fit.","Tested registration, recent, remote and semantic memory, then delayed recall.","Tested comprehension, repetition and graded naming.","Tested reading and writing.","Tested the upper quadrants of the visual fields.","Localised the findings and advised repeat testing after the postictal period."],"pitfalls":["Forgetting to give the address early, so there is no delayed recall.","Naming only common objects, which misses mild naming difficulty.","Calling it dementia on the day of a seizure.","Not mentioning driving."],"refs":["1. NICE NG217 Epilepsies in children, young people and adults (2022; checked Nov 2026)","2. DVLA. Assessing fitness to drive: a guide for medical professionals (checked Nov 2026)","3. Hodges JR. Cognitive Assessment for Clinicians, 3rd ed. Oxford University Press, 2017 (checked Nov 2026)","4. GMC. Confidentiality: patients' fitness to drive and reporting concerns to the DVLA (2017; checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: liaison teams in the UK are often asked 'is it dementia?' after a seizure, delirium or a fall. Give a careful baseline, say exactly what might be temporary, and book a repeat.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"}
);
